1
00:01:06,236 --> 00:01:08,726
New Orleans,
good morning. It's Rob Ryan in with you.

2
00:01:09,238 --> 00:01:11,138
Coming up, tickets for the big
Voodoo Fest coming to town...

3
00:01:11,173 --> 00:01:12,902
...Ponchartrain,
to the banks of the Mississippi,

4
00:01:13,576 --> 00:01:15,642
from Baton Rouge to Bayou Boutin,

5
00:01:15,677 --> 00:01:17,304
this is Hooks
checking your news headlines.

6
00:01:17,679 --> 00:01:19,509
Good day to be in New Orleans...

7
00:01:24,083 --> 00:01:26,745
How's the birthday boy? Huh?

8
00:01:30,422 --> 00:01:32,857
- And this is for Mommy.
- Thank you, sweetheart.

9
00:01:32,892 --> 00:01:35,848
Look at Grandpa.
Look at Grandpa. Say hi.

10
00:01:38,228 --> 00:01:40,957
- You wanna open a present?
- Yeah!

11
00:01:45,534 --> 00:01:47,661
- Good morning, Mr. Wood.
- Morning, Katie.

12
00:01:48,103 --> 00:01:50,169
- Paper.
- Oh.

13
00:01:50,204 --> 00:01:52,900
- Thank you. How was your weekend?
- Great. How was yours?

14
00:01:53,374 --> 00:01:54,840
- Very nice.
- Good.

15
00:01:55,008 --> 00:01:56,373
- How are the kids?
- Great.

16
00:01:56,543 --> 00:01:57,407
Oh, sh...

17
00:01:57,611 --> 00:02:00,477
Henry!

18
00:02:00,679 --> 00:02:02,203
Jacob.

19
00:02:02,381 --> 00:02:04,246
- Ted. Morning.
- It's happening.

20
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:06,314
- What? What's happening?
- Does your car have a radio?

21
00:02:06,517 --> 00:02:08,519
Greenspan's speech
was delayed after the FOMC meeting.

22
00:02:08,554 --> 00:02:10,315
Rumor is he's sick,
and my screen looks like

23
00:02:10,454 --> 00:02:12,081
the end of the world
is factored into prices.

24
00:02:12,289 --> 00:02:14,723
Let's have everyone in
the conference room in ten minutes.

25
00:02:15,157 --> 00:02:17,022
Deborah, I need you for five minutes.

26
00:02:17,226 --> 00:02:19,562
Ted, it's Monday, buddy.
There'll be a Tuesday.

27
00:02:19,597 --> 00:02:21,620
OK,
you have a conference call

28
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:24,294
about the anti-trust approvals
with Hallory at 11.

29
00:02:24,633 --> 00:02:27,533
Close the door, please.

30
00:02:30,304 --> 00:02:34,637
- So, how did Henry's party go?
- Terrific. He had a great time.

31
00:02:35,208 --> 00:02:37,903
Good.

32
00:02:38,110 --> 00:02:42,808
- I-I have a problem.
- OK. What is it?

33
00:02:56,457 --> 00:02:58,891
He taught me a song for his birthday,
and I have to sing it for him

34
00:02:59,327 --> 00:03:02,090
tonight at bedtime, and I can't think,
for the life of me, what it is.

35
00:03:02,125 --> 00:03:04,854
OK, OK. Just, you know, sing me a little
and we'll figure it out together.

36
00:03:20,678 --> 00:03:23,077
Whoo!

37
00:03:23,312 --> 00:03:26,543
What the hell is this?

38
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:32,012
Call 911!

39
00:03:32,153 --> 00:03:34,849
- 911.
- Yes, I wanna report a shooting.

40
00:03:44,096 --> 00:03:45,222
- Get behind the desk.
- What?

41
00:03:45,364 --> 00:03:47,422
- Get behind the desk and stay there!
- Ma'am?

42
00:03:47,565 --> 00:03:48,998
Uh, the Laurel-Morgan Building.

43
00:03:49,033 --> 00:03:51,194
- Third floor, Pete Murray and Colfax.
- How many are injured, ma'am?

44
00:03:51,703 --> 00:03:53,135
- Ma'am?
- I don't know.

45
00:03:53,170 --> 00:03:55,661
I don't know how many...
No, you gotta stay with me! God!

46
00:03:56,106 --> 00:03:57,663
- Quiet...
- They put me on hold.

47
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:59,840
- Hello?
- Oh, shit.

48
00:03:59,875 --> 00:04:02,343
- Deborah. Deborah, calm down.
- Can you see anything?

49
00:04:02,911 --> 00:04:08,075
- It's gonna be fine. I promise.
- Hello?

50
00:04:08,215 --> 00:04:11,742
Hello? Come on...

51
00:04:16,856 --> 00:04:21,121
Come on.
Please send somebody.

52
00:04:21,226 --> 00:04:24,285
Somebody pick up.
Please pick up.

53
00:04:59,490 --> 00:05:03,017
- Good morning, Mr. Pulaski.
- Morning, Nick.

54
00:05:04,494 --> 00:05:07,725
- Do you need some help with that?
- Ah, bilge ring keeps crappin' out,

55
00:05:07,997 --> 00:05:10,191
blocking up the damn pump.
I got it now, Nick.

56
00:05:10,499 --> 00:05:12,490
Last time, you nearly took out
every sink in the quarter, you know.

57
00:05:12,768 --> 00:05:17,261
Hey, that was those kids messing
with the water main.

58
00:05:17,404 --> 00:05:19,200
You know, you should
really quit those things.

59
00:05:19,439 --> 00:05:20,997
What for?

60
00:05:51,698 --> 00:05:55,133
Christmas comes a little early this year.

61
00:06:01,273 --> 00:06:04,902
Ah, there's our boy.
So, what do you say?

62
00:06:05,943 --> 00:06:07,943
Does he stop in for coffee and a cruller?

63
00:06:07,978 --> 00:06:11,708
- Ah, let's give him a cruller.
- Oh, bingo! We have a winner.

64
00:06:12,315 --> 00:06:14,582
I can smell
the fried dough over here.

65
00:06:14,617 --> 00:06:19,815
And... Ah, there he is.
Breakfast of champions, right there.

66
00:06:26,126 --> 00:06:28,693
Oh, and we're losing him.
I'll take just a couple more.

67
00:06:28,728 --> 00:06:33,858
Say good-bye to Mr. Nicholas Easter.
Who's next on the hit parade?

68
00:06:45,642 --> 00:06:47,836
- Hi.
- Bonjour.

69
00:06:49,245 --> 00:06:51,974
- Can I look at that candle?
- C'est la?

70
00:06:52,414 --> 00:06:54,714
No, I'm sorry. The red one.

71
00:06:54,749 --> 00:06:58,775
It's just to the left of that scary pentacle
and the jar of bloodroot.

72
00:06:59,253 --> 00:07:02,016
Right there?

73
00:07:03,489 --> 00:07:04,786
Oh!

74
00:07:06,159 --> 00:07:08,353
Oh, thank you. What did you say?

75
00:07:08,460 --> 00:07:10,928
She's Cajun. I told her that
you wanted the St. Nicholas candle.

76
00:07:10,963 --> 00:07:14,193
- Right? Is that what you wanted?
- Yeah, that's the one I was pointing to.

77
00:07:17,868 --> 00:07:20,302
Now, what happened right there?
What did she say?

78
00:07:20,337 --> 00:07:23,499
She said you're a nice-looking boy,
but she doesn't trust you.

79
00:07:24,006 --> 00:07:26,406
She didn't say that, did she?

80
00:07:26,575 --> 00:07:29,042
You know what it is?

81
00:07:29,210 --> 00:07:34,548
Jury duty. Got a summons in the mail.
Just trying to pray my way out of it.

82
00:07:34,583 --> 00:07:38,643
- Where's your sense of civic duty?
- Um, I flunked civics.

83
00:07:39,185 --> 00:07:42,211
- Oh.
- Do you have any other tips?

84
00:07:42,355 --> 00:07:45,983
I would go with the St. Catherine candle.

85
00:07:46,191 --> 00:07:48,215
- What did she do?
- She's the patron saint

86
00:07:48,359 --> 00:07:50,793
of unmarried women and... jurors.

87
00:07:50,961 --> 00:07:52,760
- Really?
- Really.

88
00:07:52,795 --> 00:07:55,161
That's not what that candle really is,
is it?

89
00:08:11,478 --> 00:08:13,878
This your first time in New Orleans?

90
00:08:13,913 --> 00:08:16,143
No, no. I've been here
a number of times.

91
00:08:16,482 --> 00:08:20,781
Hm. I had you pegged as a first-timer.

92
00:08:20,919 --> 00:08:24,376
- Is that right?
- Yeah.

93
00:08:31,427 --> 00:08:33,727
- How's your mother?
- Excuse me?

94
00:08:33,762 --> 00:08:37,960
- Is she feeling better?
- How do you know about my mother?

95
00:08:38,233 --> 00:08:41,360
She was in the hospital, but now
you're taking care of her at home.

96
00:08:41,735 --> 00:08:43,736
She had a stroke.

97
00:08:43,770 --> 00:08:45,567
Your wife wants you
to put her in a home,

98
00:08:45,872 --> 00:08:49,068
but you're feeling guilty about that.
You've checked out a few of them,

99
00:08:49,441 --> 00:08:52,274
and it doesn't feel like
the Christian thing to do.

100
00:08:52,444 --> 00:08:55,935
My advice: Reconsider the home.

101
00:08:56,113 --> 00:08:59,673
Better an unhappy mother
than an unfriendly wife.

102
00:09:01,952 --> 00:09:03,476
I'm up here on the right.

103
00:09:07,389 --> 00:09:10,089
The same thing with guns
and Vicksburg Firearms.

104
00:09:10,124 --> 00:09:13,491
The only thing that's gonna win the case
for us is because for the first time,

105
00:09:13,761 --> 00:09:17,321
we got a gun company ex-executive who's
gonna go up against his former employer

106
00:09:17,864 --> 00:09:21,801
and, with good conscience,
he's gonna testify for us.

107
00:09:21,836 --> 00:09:24,463
I promised Celeste
that I would win it for her

108
00:09:25,136 --> 00:09:27,263
and all the other victims
that died that day.

109
00:09:27,339 --> 00:09:28,939
You're gonna make history, Celeste.

110
00:09:28,974 --> 00:09:32,841
We're gonna make history together.
Thank you for your courage.

111
00:09:40,749 --> 00:09:43,240
Barry, we got
the Strickland SUV case at noon,

112
00:09:43,652 --> 00:09:46,216
and, Celeste, I'm gonna see you
and your son at Arnaud's at six...

113
00:09:46,251 --> 00:09:48,781
- Just as long as we don't talk about...
- Nothing about the case.

114
00:09:49,056 --> 00:09:52,286
This is dinner with you and Henry.
Barry, you got time to see her to her car?

115
00:09:52,692 --> 00:09:54,421
- Absolutely.
- Thank you.

116
00:09:54,627 --> 00:09:56,287
- Mr. Rohr.
- Yeah?

117
00:09:56,495 --> 00:09:58,861
Lawrence Green. Lindus Hostetler.

118
00:09:59,031 --> 00:09:59,964
- Ah, Hostetler.
- We... Yeah.

119
00:09:59,999 --> 00:10:02,728
- Yes, jury consultants, New York City.
- Philadelphia.

120
00:10:03,001 --> 00:10:04,491
I wanna thank you for agreeing
to meet with me, sir. Thank you.

121
00:10:05,003 --> 00:10:07,300
I just want you to guess
how many phone calls and letters

122
00:10:07,504 --> 00:10:10,507
I get from expert jury consultants
such as yourself.

123
00:10:10,542 --> 00:10:11,996
- I can imagine, sir.
- Go on, take a guess.

124
00:10:12,508 --> 00:10:14,840
Well, I appreciate that
you invited me down here, sir.

125
00:10:15,011 --> 00:10:17,706
Well, this is more important now.
Which one?

126
00:10:17,946 --> 00:10:19,479
- I'm sorry?
- Well, I'm off to court.

127
00:10:19,514 --> 00:10:21,640
This is the striped, non-striped.
Striped, non-striped.

128
00:10:22,082 --> 00:10:24,710
Go... I would go with the striped one, sir.
It matches your jacket.

129
00:10:25,019 --> 00:10:26,417
And you have a striped one
because you're from New York City.

130
00:10:26,586 --> 00:10:27,780
Philadelphia, sir.

131
00:10:28,021 --> 00:10:29,613
I think we're gonna go with
the non-striped one, Phyllis.

132
00:10:29,756 --> 00:10:31,989
You know, as a clever jury consultant,

133
00:10:32,024 --> 00:10:34,254
you should know that jurors down here
don't trust a lawyer

134
00:10:34,493 --> 00:10:36,892
- who's too nattily turned out. Huh?
- It's...

135
00:10:37,028 --> 00:10:39,553
- How familiar are you with my case?
- Very, sir.

136
00:10:40,031 --> 00:10:41,997
- You think it's a strong case?
- Yes, sir.

137
00:10:42,032 --> 00:10:46,627
- Ooh. Do you think it's a winning case?
- People don't win gun cases, Mr. Rohr.

138
00:10:47,970 --> 00:10:49,835
I think I can help you with that, sir.

139
00:10:51,672 --> 00:10:53,367
There's gotta be
another commuter flight.

140
00:10:53,674 --> 00:10:55,972
Have you tried one
in Richmond or Atlanta?

141
00:10:57,811 --> 00:11:00,473
I gotta call you back. He's here.

142
00:11:02,548 --> 00:11:05,881
You represent the widow of a man
shot by a Vicksburg Firearms gun.

143
00:11:06,052 --> 00:11:08,349
That gun company's finding out
things about prospective jurors

144
00:11:08,553 --> 00:11:10,055
their husbands and their wives
don't even know.

145
00:11:10,090 --> 00:11:11,920
- Is that right?
- Yeah. And the word is,

146
00:11:12,556 --> 00:11:16,686
the defense has retained Rankin Fitch
as their lead jury consultant.

147
00:11:16,794 --> 00:11:18,784
- Rankin Fitch?
- You don't...

148
00:11:18,928 --> 00:11:20,122
No, I know who he is.

149
00:11:23,532 --> 00:11:25,693
Mr. Fitch, how was your flight?

150
00:11:25,734 --> 00:11:27,826
Fine. Are we up and running,
Miss Monroe?

151
00:11:28,069 --> 00:11:32,239
We are. Kaufman's inside with Birk
and the kid from MIT, Lamb, he's here, too.

152
00:11:32,274 --> 00:11:35,105
- What about Broussard, Forensic...
- Linguistics out of Rochester.

153
00:11:35,708 --> 00:11:38,471
He missed his connecting flight
in Atlanta, but he'll be here by three.

154
00:11:38,644 --> 00:11:40,441
- Who's our backup?
- Sir, he'll be here by three.

155
00:11:40,646 --> 00:11:43,112
- Who is our backup?
- Levon Raines, out of Dallas.

156
00:11:43,147 --> 00:11:46,878
All right. Call Mr. Broussard.
Tell him his service is no longer required.

157
00:11:48,385 --> 00:11:51,286
- See what time Raines can be here.
- Yes, sir.

158
00:11:51,888 --> 00:11:53,445
Mr. Fitch.

159
00:12:01,262 --> 00:12:03,627
I just want you to tell me
why I need a jury consultant.

160
00:12:04,198 --> 00:12:06,723
Fitch's team will be scientifically
picking jurors predisposed in his favor

161
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:10,703
by using video surveillance,
wiretaps, psych profiles, graphology...

162
00:12:10,738 --> 00:12:12,261
- Barry, where's my car?
- It's where you parked it.

163
00:12:12,505 --> 00:12:13,698
All right, come on. We're going together.

164
00:12:14,005 --> 00:12:17,340
Sir, I have a master's in psychology
from the University of Chicago.

165
00:12:17,375 --> 00:12:21,105
I know handwriting analysis. I know
this stuff. I really do. Plus civics, OK?

166
00:12:23,547 --> 00:12:25,713
Delores Kinnerly,
registered Democrat,

167
00:12:25,748 --> 00:12:30,343
46 years of age, unmarried,
occupational therapist. Devout cat lover.

168
00:12:30,853 --> 00:12:33,622
Yeah, look at the way she turns away
from the man walking his dog.

169
00:12:33,657 --> 00:12:35,680
She's definitely self-conscious
about her weight.

170
00:12:36,190 --> 00:12:38,385
Maybe she just hates dogs.

171
00:12:39,426 --> 00:12:40,791
- Good morning.
- Morning.

172
00:12:40,961 --> 00:12:42,826
Freeze that, Mr. Lamb.

173
00:12:46,532 --> 00:12:48,397
We love fat women, people.

174
00:12:50,201 --> 00:12:52,499
They're tight-fisted, unsympathetic.

175
00:12:52,637 --> 00:12:56,003
I want her on my jury. Ladies and
gentlemen, let's find 11 more jurors

176
00:12:56,173 --> 00:12:57,439
and three alternates just like her.

177
00:12:57,474 --> 00:13:00,340
- Who's next?
- Jerry Fernandez, 40 years old.

178
00:13:00,943 --> 00:13:02,945
Puerto Rican. Works for
a local moving company...

179
00:13:02,980 --> 00:13:05,139
I got 35 years
of experience in courtrooms,

180
00:13:05,413 --> 00:13:06,413
and my instincts for picking jurors

181
00:13:06,448 --> 00:13:09,440
has served me quite nicely so far.
I wanna thank you for your time.

182
00:13:09,984 --> 00:13:13,215
Mr. Rohr, I flew down here
on my own dime.

183
00:13:13,354 --> 00:13:15,120
- Why?
- Because I... I...

184
00:13:15,155 --> 00:13:18,056
Because I believe in...
I believe in this case!

185
00:13:18,658 --> 00:13:23,356
- Why?
- I... I believe in a world without guns.

186
00:13:23,562 --> 00:13:26,325
That's very sweet, but a little naive.

187
00:13:30,400 --> 00:13:33,602
- 30 percent.
- I'm sorry. Excuse me?

188
00:13:33,637 --> 00:13:38,096
30 percent of your usual fee,
you're hired, strictly probationary status.

189
00:13:38,174 --> 00:13:39,334
You don't wanna check out my resumé

190
00:13:39,842 --> 00:13:42,639
Already did. It's impressive.

191
00:13:45,812 --> 00:13:47,837
- OK, here's the thing.
- What've you got?

192
00:13:48,482 --> 00:13:49,847
"The following
are automatically exempt... "

193
00:13:49,983 --> 00:13:52,177
- I read through that stuff.
- "Not a resident of New Orleans Parish. "

194
00:13:52,318 --> 00:13:53,444
That's not gonna work.
What am I gonna say?

195
00:13:53,819 --> 00:13:55,421
- We have to be thorough.
- Russell, I'm gonna tell these people

196
00:13:55,456 --> 00:13:57,820
- I'm just gonna pack up and leave?
- Number two...

197
00:13:57,856 --> 00:13:59,847
What do you mean, pack up and leave?
I've seen your stuff.

198
00:14:00,358 --> 00:14:01,824
- You ain't got nothing to pack.
- No, it's true. They own you.

199
00:14:01,859 --> 00:14:04,521
But I'll tell you this. One thing
you can do: Leave the country.

200
00:14:04,862 --> 00:14:06,726
Why am I gonna leave the country?

201
00:14:06,930 --> 00:14:09,057
- Uh, church convention.
- Sex change.

202
00:14:09,199 --> 00:14:11,189
You're leaving because
you're becoming a woman.

203
00:14:11,333 --> 00:14:12,994
Yeah. You got a goat farm...

204
00:14:13,202 --> 00:14:16,205
This whole thing is a disaster
because I got my life, I got my work

205
00:14:16,239 --> 00:14:18,604
and I got my training.
The Challenge. The Challenge.

206
00:14:19,006 --> 00:14:21,769
- Ooh! Ooh! You can claim hardship.
- That's eight weeks away...

207
00:14:22,008 --> 00:14:23,339
- Eight weeks and you're gone.
- I can't do it.

208
00:14:23,743 --> 00:14:24,539
And you can't do it.

209
00:14:24,778 --> 00:14:26,506
You've got the Challenge. You can't go.

210
00:14:26,679 --> 00:14:30,342
All right, all right. Check this. "If you
have been convicted of a felony... "

211
00:14:30,549 --> 00:14:33,179
- Have you been convicted of a felony?
- No.

212
00:14:33,214 --> 00:14:35,811
You should commit a felony.
Just commit a crime.

213
00:14:36,054 --> 00:14:38,749
- Are you serious?
- It doesn't have to be a bad crime.

214
00:14:39,122 --> 00:14:41,625
- You're out. You're gone.
- I should commit a crime?

215
00:14:41,660 --> 00:14:43,489
You're asking to get out of it,
are you not?

216
00:14:44,060 --> 00:14:45,826
You registered to vote.
That's the problem.

217
00:14:45,861 --> 00:14:49,193
If you register to vote,
then they got you in that jury pool.

218
00:14:49,631 --> 00:14:51,360
We need to de-register him.

219
00:14:51,566 --> 00:14:54,295
You know, one of these days, I'm gonna
get organized, throw half this stuff away.

220
00:14:54,368 --> 00:14:57,427
Oh, what's that?
A Walther PPK. That's a nice piece.

221
00:14:57,704 --> 00:14:59,671
That's the same gun James Bond uses.
You know that?

222
00:14:59,706 --> 00:15:04,301
Really? Can't be too safe these days.
Walking on the streets all alone.

223
00:15:04,876 --> 00:15:06,673
- Smooth, Maxine.
- Tell you what.

224
00:15:06,878 --> 00:15:09,539
It's easier to find an honest firearm
these days than a good man.

225
00:15:09,747 --> 00:15:11,339
- Ouch!
- You know how to use it?

226
00:15:11,549 --> 00:15:13,014
Oh, my brother took me out
