- Ex-Marine drill instructor.

450
00:27:23,970 --> 00:27:27,340
These people are looking for a leader.
They'll follow Frank Herrera.

451
00:27:27,375 --> 00:27:28,705
And if they elect someone else foreman?

452
00:27:28,975 --> 00:27:31,841
Whoever they vote for will be following me.

453
00:27:32,311 --> 00:27:35,370
Just like in Cincinnati
and Oakland and Pittsburgh.

454
00:27:35,513 --> 00:27:42,042
Gentlemen, trials are too important
to be left up to juries.

455
00:27:44,854 --> 00:27:47,948
God save this state
and this honorable court.

456
00:27:48,257 --> 00:27:50,281
Good morning, ladies and gentlemen.
You may take your seats.

457
00:27:50,658 --> 00:27:52,717
- Who's next?
- Herman Grimes.

458
00:27:52,861 --> 00:27:54,328
Who? Who'd did he say?

459
00:27:54,362 --> 00:27:55,419
Is there a Herman Grimes
in the court?

460
00:27:55,696 --> 00:27:57,391
Mr. Grimes is not on my list.

461
00:28:02,168 --> 00:28:06,797
There's a swinging door
ahead of you, honey. You got it?

462
00:28:07,038 --> 00:28:10,174
45 degrees to your left,
then 20 degrees to your right.

463
00:28:10,209 --> 00:28:11,903
Uh, hold on a minute there, Mr. Grimes.

464
00:28:12,543 --> 00:28:14,678
There's no need for you
to have come down here today.

465
00:28:14,713 --> 00:28:16,474
You're excused for reasons of disability.

466
00:28:17,013 --> 00:28:18,503
And what if I do not
wish to be excused, sir?

467
00:28:18,681 --> 00:28:22,673
Well... I've never known
a blind man to serve on a jury,

468
00:28:22,885 --> 00:28:25,187
and I must say,
I can't recall any case or law

469
00:28:25,222 --> 00:28:26,517
or statute that would permit or prevent...

470
00:28:26,654 --> 00:28:30,215
State v. Jack, Your Honor.
The Louisiana Supreme Court has held

471
00:28:30,624 --> 00:28:33,855
that it is a violation of
constitutional due process

472
00:28:33,960 --> 00:28:36,223
to just automatically dismiss
the visually impaired...

473
00:28:36,729 --> 00:28:38,162
- He's right.
... from jury service.

474
00:28:38,197 --> 00:28:40,290
Well, Mr. Grimes, that's very impressive.

475
00:28:40,698 --> 00:28:44,065
Why are you smiling, sir?

476
00:28:44,202 --> 00:28:47,905
Mr. Rohr, Mr. Cable, any objections
to Mr. Grimes serving on this jury?

477
00:28:47,940 --> 00:28:51,237
The plaintiffs would welcome
Mr. Grimes to our jury, Your Honor.

478
00:28:51,807 --> 00:28:53,791
- "Our jury. "
- Mr. Cable?

479
00:28:53,826 --> 00:28:55,776
As would we on the defense, Your Honor.

480
00:28:57,379 --> 00:28:58,607
- Who's next?
- Nicholas Easter.

481
00:28:58,747 --> 00:29:00,408
Mr. Easter?

482
00:29:01,949 --> 00:29:05,248
- Mr. Rohr.
- Your Honor.

483
00:29:05,419 --> 00:29:07,181
- Good morning, Mr. Easter.
- Good morning.

484
00:29:07,387 --> 00:29:10,686
Well, it says here that you work
in an electronics store.

485
00:29:10,823 --> 00:29:11,788
Yes, sir, that's correct.

486
00:29:11,823 --> 00:29:13,120
How would you feel
if you knew there was a gun

487
00:29:13,692 --> 00:29:14,992
behind the counter in case of a robbery?

488
00:29:15,027 --> 00:29:17,654
- Well, we're in the mall, sir.
- But I think you'd agree

489
00:29:18,362 --> 00:29:20,990
that robberies take place
in malls on occasion, do they not?

490
00:29:21,132 --> 00:29:22,723
Yes, but we're in the Esplanade Mall,

491
00:29:22,933 --> 00:29:25,766
which is right across the street from
the sheriff's office in Jefferson Parish.

492
00:29:26,002 --> 00:29:27,593
Yes, I'm familiar where it is.

493
00:29:27,770 --> 00:29:31,103
Well, I mean, you'd have to be on crack
to try to rob it. I guess that's my point.

494
00:29:31,273 --> 00:29:34,332
I see. Well, tell me...

495
00:29:34,409 --> 00:29:35,706
Mr. Easter, that's the second time
you've looked at your watch.

496
00:29:35,944 --> 00:29:37,209
Are we keeping you from something?

497
00:29:37,244 --> 00:29:39,144
No, Your Honor.
I wouldn't presume to waste your time.

498
00:29:39,746 --> 00:29:41,578
I just... I think if you knew my situation,

499
00:29:41,613 --> 00:29:43,410
you might be inclined to excuse me from...

500
00:29:44,016 --> 00:29:46,610
- Your situation, Mr. Easter?
- Yes, Your Honor.

501
00:29:49,287 --> 00:29:52,021
- It's the Madden Challenge.
- The Madden what?

502
00:29:52,056 --> 00:29:54,754
You know, "Madden Football. "
It's a video game.

503
00:29:55,259 --> 00:29:58,056
Well, you'd be surprised at
how many people play. At any rate,

504
00:29:58,261 --> 00:30:00,763
every year, they choose the best
15 players from across the country

505
00:30:00,798 --> 00:30:02,025
to compete in a kind of
tournament, you know,

506
00:30:05,300 --> 00:30:05,595
- against NFL players and celebrities...
- I'm not following you.

507
00:30:05,801 --> 00:30:09,133
It's like this, Your Honor: The prize
is $100,000. So I just don't know

508
00:30:09,203 --> 00:30:12,473
if I can afford to pass up the opportunity
to make that kind of cash. It's a big deal.

509
00:30:12,508 --> 00:30:14,269
I'd like this boy to have
a lesson in civic responsibility.

510
00:30:14,407 --> 00:30:15,635
Do you have any objection
to that, Mr. Rohr?

511
00:30:16,276 --> 00:30:17,277
Your Honor, it's something
I've been doing for a very long time.

512
00:30:17,312 --> 00:30:19,267
The jury system
was originated, Mr. Easter,

513
00:30:19,478 --> 00:30:21,844
because, for thousands of years
before that, an individual judge

514
00:30:21,914 --> 00:30:26,509
had the power to hang, for example,
any young man he simply did not like.

515
00:30:28,719 --> 00:30:30,986
- Yes, Your Honor.
- Any objections, Mr. Rohr?

516
00:30:31,021 --> 00:30:33,252
I have no objection whatsoever,
Your Honor.

517
00:30:33,856 --> 00:30:35,153
- Mr. Cable?
- What do I do?

518
00:30:35,324 --> 00:30:37,849
- Any time today, counselor.
- Harkin sandbagged us.

519
00:30:37,994 --> 00:30:42,327
- Go ahead and take him. Take him.
- Uh, no objections, Your Honor.

520
00:30:42,397 --> 00:30:45,092
- Enter Mr. Nicholas Easter as a juror.
- Freeze that right there, will ya?

521
00:30:45,466 --> 00:30:47,525
Freeze it.

522
00:31:07,517 --> 00:31:10,815
30 years in the trenches
have taught me this:

523
00:31:11,020 --> 00:31:13,215
There's always a loose end.

524
00:31:13,355 --> 00:31:17,848
Everybody has a secret
they don't want you to find. Find it.

525
00:31:18,026 --> 00:31:22,223
First group, Ms. Monroe: Rikki Coleman,
Sylvia Deshazo, Vanessa Lembeck.

526
00:31:22,362 --> 00:31:26,894
Mr. Klein: Herman Grimes,
Millie Dupree, Eddie Weese.

527
00:31:26,929 --> 00:31:31,427
Mr. Garson: Loreen Duke,
Lonnie Shaver, Frank Herrera.

528
00:31:32,037 --> 00:31:35,836
Mr. Lamb: The three alternates.
Lydia Deets, yoga teacher,

529
00:31:36,040 --> 00:31:39,237
Phillip Seville, shrimp fisherman.
Henry Wu.

530
00:31:39,376 --> 00:31:40,672
Let's find out everything we don't know.

531
00:31:40,877 --> 00:31:43,072
Mr. Doyle, you know the city
better than anyone.

532
00:31:43,179 --> 00:31:46,964
You take Jerry Fernandez,
Stella Hullic, Nicholas Easter.

533
00:31:46,999 --> 00:31:50,749
These are your babies, people.
This is what you live for.

534
00:32:26,647 --> 00:32:28,512
I think you got the wrong apartment.

535
00:32:32,718 --> 00:32:36,244
What are you gonna do?
Call the cops? Hm?

536
00:32:43,594 --> 00:32:47,029
- You weren't there today.
- I couldn't. I was too nervous.

537
00:32:47,430 --> 00:32:49,864
What are you gonna do?
You gonna hit me in the head with my...

538
00:32:50,033 --> 00:32:53,058
my George Foster big stick?

539
00:32:53,202 --> 00:32:55,762
What are you nervous about, huh?

540
00:32:55,904 --> 00:32:59,236
You are not juror number nine
in the biggest case of the year.

541
00:32:59,440 --> 00:33:01,703
You're kidding. You're kidding me!

542
00:33:01,741 --> 00:33:05,412
- You're kidding me!
- No, I'm not kidding you.

543
00:33:05,447 --> 00:33:07,811
- How'd you do it?
- Let go of that thing.

544
00:33:08,247 --> 00:33:11,045
- How'd you do it?
- Madden Challenge.

545
00:33:11,250 --> 00:33:15,345
It was a thing of beauty.
The judge even threatened to hang me.

546
00:33:17,254 --> 00:33:19,552
- Juror number nine.
- Juror number nine.

547
00:33:19,957 --> 00:33:23,722
What do you have for me?

548
00:33:29,164 --> 00:33:31,462
I love St. Catherine.

549
00:34:14,400 --> 00:34:18,735
- How do I look?
- Handsome.

550
00:34:18,770 --> 00:34:23,002
- Do I look honest and trustworthy?
- Hm... To an innocent eye.

551
00:34:23,507 --> 00:34:25,634
Good enough to survive
10 to 15 in the state pen?

552
00:34:25,876 --> 00:34:28,003
Don't say that. It's bad luck.

553
00:34:28,179 --> 00:34:31,670
Look... We're not gonna be
seeing each other for a while.

554
00:34:31,748 --> 00:34:34,850
They're gonna be watching us.
You have to come back to bed. Now.

555
00:34:34,885 --> 00:34:38,251
We're just gonna have to
be more careful then, aren't we?

556
00:34:43,624 --> 00:34:47,821
- How much do you think it's worth?
- 10, 15 million. Maybe more.

557
00:34:47,961 --> 00:34:49,723
Look, that's enough. Come back to bed.

558
00:34:49,962 --> 00:34:52,496
We only need eight of them for a verdict.

559
00:34:52,531 --> 00:34:55,522
Everybody's got a button.
We just gotta find out where and push.

560
00:34:55,900 --> 00:34:58,130
You have them.

561
00:34:58,269 --> 00:35:01,260
We don't have anything yet.

562
00:35:27,526 --> 00:35:30,358
...Celeste Wood.
You'll recall that two years ago,

563
00:35:30,828 --> 00:35:34,264
Wood's stockbroker husband Jacob
was gunned down in a mass slaying

564
00:35:34,298 --> 00:35:36,390
inside the brokerage firm
where he worked.

565
00:35:36,800 --> 00:35:41,566
...two years ago by a failed day-trader
who later turned the gun on himself.

566
00:35:41,704 --> 00:35:47,008
Never before has a jury found
the gun industry liable for murder.

567
00:35:47,043 --> 00:35:49,342
You might remember also
the former mayor of the city...

568
00:35:49,377 --> 00:35:51,469
...after her husband was gunned down
in the prime of his life.

569
00:35:51,845 --> 00:35:54,414
The man leading this: Famed
New Orleans attorney Wendall Rohr...

570
00:35:54,449 --> 00:35:57,712
Mr. Cable, do you feel
that this lawsuit is frivolous?

571
00:35:58,417 --> 00:36:02,011
Uh, it's capricious. I find
it's unfortunate that Mr. Rohr has chosen

572
00:36:02,254 --> 00:36:04,381
to use Miss Wood
and the courts as vehicles...

573
00:36:04,522 --> 00:36:06,386
Mr. Rohr, how do you
expect to win this case?

574
00:36:06,624 --> 00:36:07,716
It's very simple.

575
00:36:07,858 --> 00:36:10,559
Reckless, profit-hungry gun companies
like Vicksburg

576
00:36:10,594 --> 00:36:14,461
have been getting away
with murder for too long, and we have...

577
00:36:30,910 --> 00:36:32,740
I'm losing a lot of money
on this jury thing.

578
00:36:32,945 --> 00:36:35,175
Oh, really?

579
00:36:35,314 --> 00:36:36,473
- What?
- 16.

580
00:36:36,614 --> 00:36:37,842
- 16?
- Yeah.

581
00:36:37,949 --> 00:36:41,251
No way. $16 a day?
What is this, 1892?

582
00:36:41,286 --> 00:36:43,515
Well, you're not supposed to
do it for the pay, darling.

583
00:36:43,553 --> 00:36:44,884
You're supposed to do it out of civic duty.

584
00:36:45,055 --> 00:36:48,148
- Nice to meet you. Frank Herrera.
- Henry Wu. I became a citizen...

585
00:36:48,624 --> 00:36:52,025
Well, he deserves it.
He's the president of the free world.

586
00:36:52,060 --> 00:36:55,325
There's coffee and donuts over there.
Now, these muffins, I made 'em myself.

587
00:36:55,797 --> 00:36:58,390
A treat for the first day.

588
00:36:59,833 --> 00:37:02,096
These look like they are a treat.

589
00:37:06,439 --> 00:37:09,805
OK. Morning.

590
00:37:09,975 --> 00:37:14,043
Well, everyone's here.
All accounted for? Good.

591
00:37:14,078 --> 00:37:17,308
I think we should elect a jury foreman,
like Judge Harkin instructed us.

592
00:37:17,647 --> 00:37:18,947
And who would that be, Mr. Man?

593
00:37:18,982 --> 00:37:22,781
Well, if no one else feels strongly about it,
I'd like to throw my hat in the ring.

594
00:37:23,385 --> 00:37:25,519
Uh-uh, excuse me...

595
00:37:25,554 --> 00:37:27,317
- Mister...
- Herrera, ma'am. Frank Herrera.

596
00:37:28,022 --> 00:37:31,014
Mr. Herrera. Out of all of us,

597
00:37:31,059 --> 00:37:33,891
what makes you think
you're the best person for the job?

598
00:37:34,227 --> 00:37:37,321
- Well, Miss...
- Mrs. Loreen Duke.

599
00:37:37,464 --> 00:37:38,828
- Mrs. Duke.
- Mm-hm.

600
00:37:38,998 --> 00:37:40,898
I was a master sergeant
in the Marine Corps.

601
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:43,365
I served my country
in Panama, Grenada, Beirut.

602
00:37:43,635 --> 00:37:45,398
- You name it, I was there.
- I can run a 6-minute mile.

603
00:37:45,603 --> 00:37:46,467
Young lady...

604
00:37:46,671 --> 00:37:49,138
Well, if nobody minds, I'd also
like to be considered for foreman.

605
00:37:49,173 --> 00:37:52,343
I mean, I might not have
served in Grenada or Panama,

606
00:37:52,378 --> 00:37:54,173
but I'm the mother of two small children...

607
00:37:54,811 --> 00:37:57,347
In that case, y'all ought to consider me.
Hell, I got three kids.

608
00:37:57,382 --> 00:38:00,645
Ladies and gentlemen, with all due
respect,

609
00:38:00,949 --> 00:38:02,678
I think I know who would
make an excellent foreman.

610
00:38:03,185 --> 00:38:06,587
- Oh, and who might that be, Mister...
- Easter. Nicholas Easter, sir.

611
00:38:06,622 --> 00:38:09,020
I was watching a lot of people very closely

612
00:38:09,422 --> 00:38:13,188
during the jury selection, and only
this man... I don't know your name, sir.

613
00:38:13,460 --> 00:38:15,586
- What is your name?
- Herman Grimes.

614
00:38:15,828 --> 00:38:18,058
- Only Mr. Grimes...
- No, just Herman.

615
00:38:18,197 --> 00:38:20,221
Only Herman had the guts
to stand up to the judge,

616
00:38:20,365 --> 00:38:23,664
and only Herman seems to
know a little something about the law.

617
00:38:23,834 --> 00:38:28,066
So, in my book, you know, I think it'd be
a good idea for him to represent us.

618
00:38:28,205 --> 00:38:29,899
- But...
- But he's blind, man. So what?

619
00:38:30,172 --> 00:38:33,309
- So is justice, right?
- That's right, brother.

620
00:38:33,344 --> 00:38:34,707
What do you think, Herman?
Are you up to it?

621
00:38:35,377 --> 00:38:38,710
- Well, of course I am.
- All in favor of Herman.

622
00:38:48,187 --> 00:38:51,390
- Did I get any votes?
- Hey, it's unanimous. You're in.

623
00:38:51,425 --> 00:38:54,517
- Congratulations.
- All right, listen up.

624
00:38:54,825 --> 00:38:57,123
Court's in session. Lunch is set for 1:00.

625
00:38:57,528 --> 00:39:00,463
We order out from Verdi's Mart.
Please fill out a menu.

626
00:39:00,498 --> 00:39:03,159
Make sure you circle what you want, now.

627
00:39:03,700 --> 00:39:06,166
Don't make it too expensive. Thank you.

628
00:39:06,201 --> 00:39:09,261
Oh, I had this last week.
It's really good. Thank you.

629
00:39:09,505 --> 00:39:11,131
- Think I'm gonna have salmon.
- Nothing on that menu.

630
00:39:11,172 --> 00:39:13,367
All right, all right. Thank you.

631
00:39:19,045 --> 00:39:23,849
October, two years ago,
at 8:45 am on a Monday morning,

632
00:39:23,884 --> 00:39:26,646
a man by the name of Kevin Peltier
casually walks though

633
00:39:26,851 --> 00:39:30,082
the brokerage house from which
he was fired the previous Friday morning.

634
00:39:30,354 --> 00:39:33,655
He walks into the elevator.
He loads a 36-round magazine

635
00:39:33,690 --> 00:39:38,217
into his Performa 990 semiautomatic,
and when he reaches the third floor,

636
00:39:38,428 --> 00:39:42,523
he opens fire on his former coworkers,
killing 11 and critically wounding five

637
00:39:42,898 --> 00:39:48,199
before turning the gun on himself.
Now, they never had a chance.

638
00:39:48,502 --> 00:39:51,271
This was all less than two minutes.
They couldn't stop him.

639
00:39:51,306 --> 00:39:55,900
11 lives ended.
That's all you jurors, minus one.

640
00:39:56,375 --> 00:39:58,468
And among them was Jacob Wood,

641
00:39:58,610 --> 00:40:00,411
the husband of my client, Celeste Wood,

642
00:40:00,446 --> 00:40:05,007
and the father of their son Henry,
six years old.

643
00:40:05,450 --> 00:40:10,079
I don't know about you, but I'm angry
at the tragic and senseless loss of life.

644
00:40:10,287 --> 00:40:13,915
Why does the Vicksburg Firearms
company make it so easy

645
00:40:14,123 --> 00:40:17,023
to buy these guns
on the underground market?

646
00:40:17,192 --> 00:40:20,821
Because they care more about
making money than they do about your life

647
00:40:20,929 --> 00:40:25,524
or my life or the life
of that woman's husband.

648
00:40:27,600 --> 00:40:30,269
A very courageous former
executive of Vicksburg Firearms

649
00:40:30,304 --> 00:40:32,463
is gonna come in here,
and he's gonna testify

650
00:40:38,876 --> 00:40:43,175
criminals,
all those others poised for violence.

651
00:40:45,548 --> 00:40:49,005
Excuse me. Would you please
give these to Mr. Rohr and Mr. Cable?

652
00:40:49,318 --> 00:40:51,616
...into a very efficient mass murderer.

653
00:40:57,091 --> 00:41:00,685
Yes, it was Mr. Peltier that squeezed
the trigger that awful Monday morning.

654
00:41:01,161 --> 00:41:03,461
But it was
the Vicksburg Firearms company,

655
00:41:03,496 --> 00:41:06,294
through a deliberate
and negligent distribution policy,

656
00:41:06,332 --> 00:41:11,064
that put that assault-type weapon
into the hands of Mr. Peltier.

657
00:41:11,669 --> 00:41:15,695
As such, they were
complicit in those murders.

658
00:41:15,839 --> 00:41:18,636
And this I'll prove to you
during the course of the trial.

659
00:41:18,841 --> 00:41:21,309
Thank you very much. Your Honor.

660
00:41:25,347 --> 00:41:30,511
- Mr. Cable.
- Thank you, Your Honor.

661
00:41:30,651 --> 00:41:32,915
- I feel so nervous.
- You'll be fine.

662
00:41:33,052 --> 00:41:35,820
That was quite provocative.

663
00:41:35,855 --> 00:41:39,255
Now, I wanna tell you
two things about guns today.

664
00:41:39,825 --> 00:41:42,659
I don't think either of them
will be news to you,

665
00:41:42,694 --> 00:41:46,754
but the first is guns are dangerous.
They're guns. This is their function.

666
00:41:47,265 --> 00:41:49,789
And I wanna tell you today
that no one is more aware of that

667
00:41:50,033 --> 00:41:52,331
or takes more responsibility for it
than the gun industry,

668
00:41:52,536 --> 00:41:55,333
especially Vicksburg Firearms,

669
00:41:55,538 --> 00:41:58,340
as a company that's been in business
since before the Civil War.

670
00:41:58,375 --> 00:42:00,899
Now, the automobile industry
works every day

671
00:42:01,342 --> 00:42:03,572
to ensure that their cars are safe.

672
00:42:03,710 --> 00:42:06,508
Why? Because they drive them.

673
00:42:06,713 --> 00:42:08,680
Now, the people at Vicksburg
work every day

674
00:42:08,715 --> 00:42:11,239
to keep the guns
out of the hands of the criminals.

675
00:42:11,684 --> 00:42:13,981
Why? Because they have
families with children...

676
00:42:14,119 --> 00:42:15,916
Did you see who handed this to you?

677
00:42:16,154 --> 00:42:18,588
Well, it was a woman. She had blond hair.

678
00:42:18,657 --> 00:42:21,458
She had pretty blond hair,
just like that woman on television.

679
00:42:21,493 --> 00:42:24,790
We didn't catch her on any of the CCTVs.

680
00:42:25,028 --> 00:42:26,995
Doesn't matter. I'm sure she was wigged.

681
00:42:27,030 --> 00:42:30,863
I doubt you'll find any, but have this
sprayed for prints. Anything turns up...

682
00:42:31,366 --> 00:42:33,891
I'll process it through the Printak database.

683
00:42:34,202 --> 00:42:38,339
- We're not here to legislate.
- Mr. Cable is winding up.

684
00:42:38,374 --> 00:42:41,205
Well, ladies and gentlemen of the jury,

685
00:42:41,908 --> 00:42:46,674
amidst all the finger-pointing,
Mr. Rohr did say one true thing:

686
00:42:46,879 --> 00:42:49,507
This case is about money.

687
00:42:49,715 --> 00:42:52,181
Hi, I'm calling from the courthouse.

688
00:42:52,216 --> 00:42:56,914
You know, it looks like Judge Harkin
won't be breaking till after two.

689
00:42:57,220 --> 00:43:01,053
Would you mind holding his jury's
lunch order till then? Thank you.

690
00:43:12,733 --> 00:43:17,737
What do you think about her? I hear
she teaches a class over at Tulane.

691
00:43:17,772 --> 00:43:19,568
- Yeah?
- Wouldn't mind takin' that class.

692
00:43:19,939 --> 00:43:22,702
- What subject?
- Yeah, well, she's a tall glass of iced tea.

693
00:43:22,941 --> 00:43:25,773
A little too much lemon,
a little too much ice.

694
00:43:28,479 --> 00:43:29,378
Um, I'm sorry,

695
00:43:29,413 --> 00:43:32,711
but would you mind not doing that
in here? I'm predisposed to asthma.

696
00:43:32,983 --> 00:43:35,185
Come on, I'll open the window.
It ain't gonna bother you.

697
00:43:35,220 --> 00:43:38,312
Actually, I mind, too.
Sorry. Secondhand smoke.

698
00:43:38,921 --> 00:43:41,189
I gotta tell you, Mr. Shaver, I don't
wanna smoke your cigarettes, either.

699
00:43:41,224 --> 00:43:44,750
Damn. I can't smoke, can't leave
the room. What is this, California?

700
00:43:45,426 --> 00:43:47,791
- It is against the rules.
- Aw, man, be quiet.

701
00:43:47,961 --> 00:43:48,794
You're just an alternate, man.

702
00:43:48,829 --> 00:43:50,296
What does that have to do with anything?

703
00:43:53,099 --> 00:43:56,590
Come on, come on.
We're gonna be here for a while together.

704
00:43:56,801 --> 00:43:59,497
So why are you bothering me?

705
00:44:02,439 --> 00:44:04,642
You know that I think?
I think we're all just really hungry.

706
00:44:04,677 --> 00:44:07,075
And where's our lunch
they promised us? It's 1:30.

707
00:44:07,477 --> 00:44:10,308
That's why I wanna smoke.
I'm starving. Tamp down my appetite.

708
00:44:10,343 --> 00:44:13,138
Herman, you want me to go
check out what's going on with lunch?

709
00:44:13,348 --> 00:44:14,815
Good idea, Nick. Go see what's taking 'em.

710
00:44:24,691 --> 00:44:26,124
So they go over there
and he sees a cow

711
00:44:26,326 --> 00:44:27,992
and he sees a golf ball
up in that cow's ass.

712
00:44:28,027 --> 00:44:30,587
And he says - well, this is delicate -
"Excuse me, ma'am. "

713
00:44:30,896 --> 00:44:33,193
And he raises that cow's tail and says,
"Does this look like yours?"

714
00:44:33,598 --> 00:44:35,862
And she hit him with a four-iron!

715
00:44:36,034 --> 00:44:37,933
- Excuse me, Judge.
- Huh?

716
00:44:37,968 --> 00:44:41,199
- There's someone to see you.
- All right, all right.

717
00:44:41,638 --> 00:44:43,468
Excuse me, gentlemen.

718
00:44:47,642 --> 00:44:49,473
- You are?
- Nick Easter, juror number nine.

719
00:44:49,644 --> 00:44:52,510
Yes. Now, what do you think
you're doing outside of that jury room,

720
00:44:52,746 --> 00:44:54,713
Mr. Easter-juror-number nine?
And where's Lou Dell?

721
00:44:54,748 --> 00:44:57,342
- She's chattin' it up in the hallway, sir.
- Well, Mr. Easter,

722
00:44:58,017 --> 00:45:00,713
I would strongly advise you to return
to that jury room and finish your lunch.

723
00:45:01,020 --> 00:45:04,089
That's the problem, sir.
I think somebody forgot to order our lunch.

724
00:45:04,124 --> 00:45:06,387
- The jury has no lunch?
- That's why I'm here, sir.

725
00:45:06,658 --> 00:45:08,090
Everybody knows you here in the parish.

726
00:45:08,559 --> 00:45:10,584
They know you like to
keep a tight schedule on your bench.

727
00:45:10,861 --> 00:45:12,726
If we're gonna be back
in less than 45 minutes...

728
00:45:12,863 --> 00:45:14,557
I gotta be honest with you, sir.
We're hungry.

729
00:45:14,597 --> 00:45:17,589
Well, what exactly,
Mr. Easter, would you like me to do?

730
00:45:18,034 --> 00:45:19,898
Hey, Lonnie, Lonnie!
Could you pass me some garlic bread?

731
00:45:20,035 --> 00:45:24,130
- You got any over there?
- Oh, absolutely, man. Stuff is delicious.

732
00:45:32,279 --> 00:45:36,476
I'm not sure, but I believe
I'm buying lunch for this jury.

733
00:45:50,593 --> 00:45:52,652
Jury's right there.

734
00:45:59,033 --> 00:46:01,763
Well, in 35 years, that is
the most absurd thing I've ever seen.

735
00:46:01,798 --> 00:46:04,493
- You got a little mustard on your tie.
- I did it on purpose.

736
00:46:04,904 --> 00:46:10,239
First day of the trial, judge is having lunch
with the jury. What's that about?

737
00:46:10,409 --> 00:46:12,434
- Look at that.
- What?

738
00:46:25,254 --> 00:46:27,722
Let's go.

739
00:46:27,923 --> 00:46:29,981
- Hey, hey, hey! To Nick!
- To Nick!

740
00:46:30,125 --> 00:46:32,559
- This is horseshit.
- It wasn't me.

741
00:46:32,627 --> 00:46:36,824
It wasn't me. It was Judge Harkin.
Judge Harkin!

742
00:46:38,432 --> 00:46:41,525
OK, that's enough.
Thank you very much. All right.

743
00:46:46,905 --> 00:46:50,704
- Here's the deposition you needed.
- Thank you, thank you.

744
00:47:00,183 --> 00:47:02,149
We gonna talk about this?

745
00:47:02,184 --> 00:47:04,675
I cannot believe you had the nerve
to bring that in this office.

746
00:47:05,154 --> 00:47:07,678
- I think we ought to discuss it at least.
- Well, a better idea:

747
00:47:07,789 --> 00:47:11,189
- Why don't you just give it to the judge?
- 'Cause they'd throw the case out.

748
00:47:11,424 --> 00:47:14,257
Let me explain something to you.
That's a defense tactic, OK?

749
00:47:14,628 --> 00:47:16,629
All it's meant to do
is just throw our focus off,

750
00:47:16,664 --> 00:47:18,596
so that we can just move toward a mistrial.

751
00:47:18,631 --> 00:47:21,827
But I'd really like it out of the office,
so give it to me, please.

752
00:47:22,400 --> 00:47:25,902
Barry, after you shred it, burn it. OK?

753
00:47:25,936 --> 00:47:27,995
You know how many of
those crank things I get?

754
00:47:28,572 --> 00:47:29,800
What if it's for real?

755
00:47:29,973 --> 00:47:32,640
- No, it's not for real.
- What if it is?

756
00:47:32,675 --> 00:47:37,668
Well, if it's for real, then this isn't a trial,
is it? Then this is just an auction,

757
00:47:38,113 --> 00:47:40,138
and I might as well get on this phone here

758
00:47:40,482 --> 00:47:43,749
and call up all those anti-gun activist
friends of mine in Washington

759
00:47:43,784 --> 00:47:48,049
and tell 'em we're just gonna put on
a telethon and we will buy this verdict.

760
00:47:51,224 --> 00:47:53,953
'Cause they're not gonna
think of that, are they?

761
00:47:57,361 --> 00:47:59,886
Somebody wanna get that phone?
You got any other suggestions?

762
00:48:00,164 --> 00:48:03,155
Well, look at it.
Syntax, handwriting, word choice.

763
00:48:03,300 --> 00:48:06,292
Clearly a woman did the note.
Someone's working the inside. A juror.

764
00:48:06,837 --> 00:48:11,102
Miss Monroe, I think seven hours
spent on this topic is sufficient.

765
00:48:11,173 --> 00:48:13,538
- Can we agree?
- Mr. Fitch, I got a call for you.

766
00:48:13,808 --> 00:48:15,639
Line seven.

767
00:48:17,244 --> 00:48:19,940
- Yes.
- Hello, Rankin.

768
00:48:20,180 --> 00:48:24,309
I'm the agent handling the property
you were notified about earlier today.

769
00:48:24,483 --> 00:48:27,815
- I'd like to know who I'm speaking with.
- You can call me Marlee.

770
00:48:27,986 --> 00:48:30,020
Pay phone, 15 miles away.

771
00:48:30,055 --> 00:48:32,750
- You can call me Marlee.
- Where are we going with this, Marlee?

772
00:48:33,357 --> 00:48:35,624
All the way to the verdict.

773
00:48:35,659 --> 00:48:39,822
The fact is, the jury's mine,
and I can push it either way. For a price.

774
00:48:40,196 --> 00:48:43,699
Sounds good. So good,
in fact, I don't believe you can do it.

775
00:48:43,734 --> 00:48:45,326
I'll bet you're old enough
to remember a time

776
00:48:45,601 --> 00:48:49,001
when people delivered on what
they promised. I'm one of those people.

777
00:48:49,503 --> 00:48:52,607
I'm also old enough to remember
Watergate, Abscam, Linda Tripp...

778
00:48:52,642 --> 00:48:54,767
...and a whole bunch of other times
people got entrapped

779
00:48:54,908 --> 00:48:57,968
for engaging in conversations
just like this, and you'd do very good

780
00:48:58,410 --> 00:49:00,644
to remember that, young lady.
There are consequences.

781
00:49:00,679 --> 00:49:05,342
Well, let me worry about those.
For now, I just hope you're feeling patriotic.

782
00:49:05,917 --> 00:49:09,147
- You hope I'm feeling...
- Patriotic?

783
00:49:10,621 --> 00:49:14,317
- Morning, Nick.
- Morning, Lou.

784
00:49:14,523 --> 00:49:17,356
- Good morning, Vanessa.
- Morning.

785
00:49:17,593 --> 00:49:18,355
- Nick.
- Hey, guys.

786
00:49:18,593 --> 00:49:19,719
- How you doin'?
- What's up?

787
00:49:19,895 --> 00:49:22,489
OK.

788
00:49:22,697 --> 00:49:24,857
- Hey, Lonnie, Sylvia.
- Hey, man.

789
00:49:25,032 --> 00:49:26,863
Good morning, Frank.

790
00:49:28,768 --> 00:49:31,134
Frank, you were in Panama
and Grenada, right?

791
00:49:31,804 --> 00:49:33,904
- And Beirut.
- Wow.

792
00:49:33,939 --> 00:49:36,203
That really must've been something, huh?

793
00:49:36,375 --> 00:49:39,036
Yeah. Pulling the bodies
of friends of mine out of the rubble.

794
00:49:39,377 --> 00:49:42,210
- It was really something, Nick.
- No, no, no, no. You don't understand.

795
00:49:42,446 --> 00:49:44,936
I had a friend who was in Desert Storm.
He was in the Corps, too.

796
00:49:44,981 --> 00:49:47,973
- What division?
- The 1-4 out of Pendleton.

797
00:49:48,418 --> 00:49:50,368
Yeah, well, that was mostly air power.

798
00:49:50,403 --> 00:49:52,319
Not much real action down there anyhow.

799
00:49:54,923 --> 00:49:57,391
Actually, there was, Frank.

800
00:49:57,592 --> 00:50:00,893
You don't know what the hell
you're talking about, Easter.

801
00:50:00,928 --> 00:50:03,522
My friend's name was Donny Rabbs,
and his helicopter was shot down

802
00:50:03,931 --> 00:50:07,428
in a Kuwaiti oil field.
He was killed 12 years ago today.

803
00:50:07,463 --> 00:50:10,925
- Sorry to hear that.
- I'm sorry. It's just a strange day.

804
00:50:11,002 --> 00:50:13,368
I always figure, if I don't remember
him today, nobody else will.

805
00:50:13,738 --> 00:50:16,103
That's the way people are. Short
memories.

806
00:50:16,507 --> 00:50:20,602
That's a terrible thing, y'all,
but we don't always know God's plan.

807
00:50:20,777 --> 00:50:24,645
Listen, I don't know if it'd be inappropriate,

808
00:50:24,781 --> 00:50:28,341
but do you think we could
do something today to remember him?

809
00:50:28,483 --> 00:50:31,485
- We could say the Lord's Prayer.
- Well, I don't wanna ask people to pray.

810
00:50:31,520 --> 00:50:34,750
- How about "God Bless America"?
- I couldn't ask people to sing.

811
00:50:35,255 --> 00:50:37,519
- I don't sing.
- I don't sing, OK?

812
00:50:39,159 --> 00:50:40,989
You know what? I have an idea.

813
00:50:41,160 --> 00:50:44,027
Good morning.
You may take your seats, please.

814
00:50:49,467 --> 00:50:51,934
I pledge allegiance

815
00:50:51,969 --> 00:50:56,735
to the flag of the United States of America

816
00:50:56,973 --> 00:51:01,341
and to the republic for which it stands,

817
00:51:01,910 --> 00:51:06,710
one nation under God, indivisible,

818
00:51:06,947 --> 00:51:10,249
with liberty and justice for all.

819
00:51:10,284 --> 00:51:13,719
I don't know about you, Wendall,
but I'm feeling pretty patriotic.

820
00:51:14,320 --> 00:51:16,810
Uh, thank you, ladies and gentlemen.
Will you please take your seats?

821
00:51:17,022 --> 00:51:18,216
Jesus.

822
00:51:24,962 --> 00:51:28,021
Shit.

823
00:51:31,667 --> 00:51:32,861
Pull!

824
00:51:41,275 --> 00:51:42,607
You wanted to see me?

825
00:51:42,642 --> 00:51:46,009
Mr. Cable called me last night
in a bit of a panic.

826
00:51:46,513 --> 00:51:50,642
Now, he's concerned that someone's
trying to monkey with our jury.

827
00:51:50,816 --> 00:51:53,978
As am I. As are my associates.

828
00:51:55,453 --> 00:51:58,285
- Who is it?
- It's a parlor game.

829
00:51:58,555 --> 00:52:01,353
Just because somebody
can influence where a jury has lunch,

830
00:52:01,558 --> 00:52:03,548
that doesn't mean
they can hand us the verdict.

831
00:52:03,659 --> 00:52:06,127
Ah, well, you see, Mr. Fitch,
this is where I get a little confused,

832
00:52:06,329 --> 00:52:08,128
because I was under the impression

833
00:52:08,163 --> 00:52:10,495
that we had already
purchased ourselves a verdict.

834
00:52:15,603 --> 00:52:17,593
You know what I love best about this gun?

835
00:52:17,871 --> 00:52:23,672
Though it's 70 years old, it's just
as reliable as the day it was made.

836
00:52:25,277 --> 00:52:27,142
Mr. Fitch!

837
00:52:28,679 --> 00:52:31,910
You find out who it is, will you?

838
00:52:34,051 --> 00:52:34,881
Pull!

839
00:52:35,118 --> 00:52:38,609
...one nation under God...

840
00:52:38,821 --> 00:52:40,254
At some point, everyone on the jury

841
00:52:40,289 --> 00:52:42,780
acknowledges Herrera,
like they're doing it for him.

842
00:52:43,124 --> 00:52:47,390
It's not him. Right here at the end,
Loreen Duke, our juror number 10...

843
00:52:47,896 --> 00:52:50,989
Right there. Freeze there.
Yeah. Tighten in on her left hand.

844
00:52:53,499 --> 00:52:56,195
Right there.

845
00:52:56,402 --> 00:52:58,426
Isn't that sweet?

846
00:52:58,570 --> 00:53:02,301
Nicholas Easter,
our song and dance man, has an agenda.

847
00:53:02,541 --> 00:53:05,941
Mr. Lamb, how did this confidence man
crash my jury pool?

848
00:53:06,043 --> 00:53:07,874
I don't know. I'd-I'd need some time, sir.

849
00:53:08,212 --> 00:53:10,270
Now. I'm asking for an educated guess.

850
00:53:10,413 --> 00:53:13,662
Maybe he just did it. I mean,
who wants jury duty? Nobody, right?

851
00:53:13,697 --> 00:53:16,911
It's like going to the dentist.
It's worse than going to the dentist.

852
00:53:16,985 --> 00:53:19,214
It's a municipal database
that nobody would ever wanna hack into.

853
00:53:19,320 --> 00:53:21,720
The security protocol is weak,
so he'd put a name on the list...

854
00:53:22,390 --> 00:53:25,085
I got it. I got it, Mr. Lamb. Thank you.

855
00:53:25,292 --> 00:53:27,590
- Mr. Doyle?
- I'm on it.

856
00:53:27,761 --> 00:53:29,092
Your Honor.
Good afternoon, Mr. Murphy.

857
00:53:29,261 --> 00:53:31,943
- You own and operate Excalibur Guns.
- Yes, sir, I do.

858
00:53:31,978 --> 00:53:34,624
Yes, and isn't Excalibur Guns
a retail outlet store

859
00:53:34,933 --> 00:53:37,629
- for Vicksburg Firearms?
- Yes, sir.

860
00:53:37,769 --> 00:53:40,035
Would you tell us about
your arrangement with a man,

861
00:53:40,070 --> 00:53:45,575
Michael Kincaid, who illegally sold
this Performa 990 semiautomatic

862
00:53:45,610 --> 00:53:48,874
to Kevin Peltier smack-dab
out of the trunk of his car?

863
00:53:49,479 --> 00:53:50,744
- My arrangement?
- Yes, sir.

864
00:53:50,779 --> 00:53:55,772
Well, Mikey... Mr. Kincaid
bought guns from my store...

865
00:53:56,283 --> 00:53:59,377
At a rate of more than 25 guns a month,
didn't you ever wonder

866
00:53:59,453 --> 00:54:02,512
who your friend Mikey was turning
around and selling these guns to?

867
00:54:02,689 --> 00:54:04,957
- Well, that's none of my business.
- Oh, you mean it's not your problem.

868
00:54:04,992 --> 00:54:07,357
- Now, hold on there. I didn't say that.
- Objection, Your Honor.

869
00:54:07,659 --> 00:54:11,185
- The counsel is testifying.
- Sustained. Mr. Rohr...

870
00:54:13,164 --> 00:54:15,222
Yo! Thanks a lot.

871
00:54:21,437 --> 00:54:23,268
- Afternoon.
- Afternoon.

872
00:54:28,276 --> 00:54:31,005
...your friend Michael Kincaid
28 guns in December.

873
00:54:31,445 --> 00:54:33,278
Now, did you ever wonder why one man

874
00:54:33,313 --> 00:54:36,008
would buy 112 guns
in a period of four months?

875
00:54:36,482 --> 00:54:39,007
- A collector?
- A collector, sir?

876
00:54:39,118 --> 00:54:43,213
112 weapons, and all of them
semiautomatic assault weapons?

877
00:54:43,488 --> 00:54:45,615
- I don't know.
- Come on, take a guess.

878
00:54:45,790 --> 00:54:48,553
- Christmas presents?
- That's a guess.

879
00:54:48,626 --> 00:54:50,787
Objection, Your Honor.
This is all speculation.

880
00:54:50,960 --> 00:54:52,621
- Sustained.
- Did they send a field rep

881
00:54:52,996 --> 00:54:55,296
- out to investigate?
- No, sir.

882
00:54:55,331 --> 00:54:57,389
No, because they were very pleased
with your success, weren't they?

883
00:54:57,533 --> 00:55:01,263
- Say again?
- Is it not true that Vicksburg Firearms,

884
00:55:01,469 --> 00:55:05,166
instead of investigating the increasing
number of guns that were being sold

885
00:55:05,406 --> 00:55:09,671
to one man, instead send you
and your wife on a trip to Jamaica.

886
00:55:10,010 --> 00:55:12,209
- No.
- No?

887
00:55:12,244 --> 00:55:15,042
My wife hates Jamaica.
We switched to Cancun.

888
00:55:17,849 --> 00:55:19,510
I have no further questions, Your Honor.

889
00:55:19,851 --> 00:55:22,910
Thank you, Mr. Rohr. We'll adjourn
today. Hold cross till tomorrow.

890
00:55:22,986 --> 00:55:25,614
Release the jury 45 minutes early.
Thank you, ladies and gentlemen.

891
00:56:07,389 --> 00:56:09,413
Oh, hi. Yeah, the landlord let me in.

892
00:56:09,457 --> 00:56:12,255
He said some of the tenants had
been having trouble with the cable.

893
00:56:12,594 --> 00:56:15,858
- Uh-huh.
- But, uh... I checked yours out and, uh...

894
00:56:27,572 --> 00:56:28,435
Hey!

895
00:57:53,508 --> 00:57:55,168
Whoa!

896
00:58:15,159 --> 00:58:16,625
Hey!

897
00:58:32,372 --> 00:58:34,704
...looks fine.
I don't think you have anything...

898
00:58:36,542 --> 00:58:39,102
- What do we got, people?
- The drive's been erased twice over.

899
00:58:39,411 --> 00:58:41,038
It's empty. But it's not zeroed out.

900
00:58:41,145 --> 00:58:43,010
He may have been
more sloppy than he thought.

901
00:58:43,348 --> 00:58:45,646
- Give me a few moments.
- Mr. Fitch.

902
00:58:45,817 --> 00:58:48,307
Lamb, trace it.

903
00:58:51,121 --> 00:58:52,815
Fitch here.

904
00:58:52,855 --> 00:58:56,517
- You don't play nice.
- What are you talking about?

905
00:58:56,858 --> 00:58:58,621
You broke into Nick Easter's apartment.

906
00:58:58,893 --> 00:59:00,986
You freely assume it was me.

907
00:59:01,162 --> 00:59:03,322
Oh, wouldn't you be
disappointed if I didn't?

908
00:59:03,464 --> 00:59:06,490
I'm bumping a juror. One of yours.

909
00:59:06,533 --> 00:59:08,898
This time it's just gonna be a pinprick.

910
00:59:09,168 --> 00:59:11,931
Next time it's really gonna hurt.

911
00:59:49,807 --> 00:59:54,800
Looks like somebody had themselves
a good time last night.

912
00:59:55,178 --> 00:59:59,513
I woke up on my stove. Oh, man.

913
00:59:59,548 --> 01:00:03,210
- You don't got any aspirin, do you?
- Uh-uh.

914
01:00:19,064 --> 01:00:21,157
I think I can do a little bit better
than an aspirin.

915
01:00:21,299 --> 01:00:23,130
- Yeah?
- Mm-hm. A little hair of the dog.

916
01:00:23,368 --> 01:00:28,531
- Oh. Can you sweeten this up for me?
- Yeah, put it under the table.

917
01:00:28,905 --> 01:00:30,736
You're an angel.

918
01:00:32,041 --> 01:00:33,599
- No, let me pour it.
- I'll pour it.

919
01:00:33,809 --> 01:00:36,107
Let me do it. Let me do it!

920
01:00:41,382 --> 01:00:44,009
It's mine, sir.

921
01:00:44,150 --> 01:00:46,846
I didn't take you for a day drinker,
Mr. Easter.

922
01:00:47,120 --> 01:00:49,417
I'm usually not.

923
01:00:52,324 --> 01:00:53,290
You still trying to get off my jury?
Is that it?

924
01:00:53,325 --> 01:00:55,724
Your Honor, I took an oath
to do my level best, and I meant it.

925
01:00:55,993 --> 01:00:58,723
Difficult as that is to believe, Mr. Easter,

926
01:00:58,996 --> 01:01:01,987
it's even more difficult
to believe this is your liquor.

927
01:01:02,099 --> 01:01:05,090
I might be wrong, but I don't think
that's your shade of lipstick.

928
01:01:07,870 --> 01:01:10,167
Ms. Hullic?

929
01:01:10,338 --> 01:01:14,042
And moving into Ms. Hullic's spot
will be first alternate Ms. Lydia Deets.

930
01:01:14,077 --> 01:01:17,067
Ms. Deets... will you join
the jurors in the box, please?

931
01:01:17,478 --> 01:01:19,345
Welcome to our jury. I trust you got

932
01:01:19,380 --> 01:01:21,074
the same instructions
the rest of the jurors did.

933
01:01:21,547 --> 01:01:22,741
- You ready to go?
- Yes, sir.

934
01:01:22,915 --> 01:01:25,178
Very good. Well...

935
01:01:25,350 --> 01:01:30,586
- Cell phone, untraceable.
- Marlee?

936
01:01:30,621 --> 01:01:32,919
- I'd like your offer, Mr. Fitch.
- Not over the phone.

937
01:01:33,090 --> 01:01:35,353
If you thought this whole thing
was gonna take place long-distance,

938
01:01:35,725 --> 01:01:36,558
you're mistaken.

939
01:01:36,593 --> 01:01:39,756
- You wanna lose another juror?
- Anybody can get a juror booted.

940
01:01:40,029 --> 01:01:41,427
That doesn't show me
that you can deliver a verdict.

941
01:01:41,530 --> 01:01:44,590
Hell, I could get Nick Easter canned
if I put five man-hours into it.

942
01:01:44,767 --> 01:01:48,827
But you won't do that. You need him
to guarantee the jury swings your way.

943
01:01:49,036 --> 01:01:51,196
So next time I call, have your offer ready.

944
01:01:56,709 --> 01:02:01,145
Ladies and gentlemen,
this girl is an amateur.

945
01:02:01,279 --> 01:02:06,978
You understand? A dilettante!
And I want this nonsense ended today!

946
01:02:07,317 --> 01:02:10,013
I want you to find her!
I want you to contain her!

947
01:02:10,387 --> 01:02:12,547
Because you're losing me my jury!

948
01:02:15,224 --> 01:02:17,691
Now find something on every one of them!

949
01:02:17,893 --> 01:02:23,125
Pull their files! Review every word,
every photo, every medical record. Do it!

950
01:02:26,766 --> 01:02:30,031
- Who do we have?
- Rikki Coleman's boyfriend's name

951
01:02:30,437 --> 01:02:33,572
is Neil Pollard. Mrs. Coleman broke it off
with him two months after the abortion.

952
01:02:33,607 --> 01:02:35,233
- Who's gonna help Daddy?
- Me, me!

953
01:02:38,476 --> 01:02:41,343
And there's no way she
would've cried on his shoulder about it.

954
01:02:41,511 --> 01:02:43,945
Your husband looks a lot different
than your boyfriend with you at the clinic.

955
01:02:44,281 --> 01:02:46,579
We'll be in touch.

956
01:02:50,553 --> 01:02:52,152
Back up, back up!

957
01:02:52,187 --> 01:02:54,451
About four months ago,
Mr. Weese began filling prescriptions

958
01:02:54,789 --> 01:02:58,656
for the following drugs:
Crixivan, Epivir, and AZT.

959
01:02:58,692 --> 01:03:02,149
Unbeknownst to his mother,
his employer, his church and his friends,

960
01:03:02,562 --> 01:03:04,655
Mr. Weese is HIV-positive.

961
01:03:04,797 --> 01:03:06,321
Get off me!

962
01:03:06,532 --> 01:03:07,998
That's what I'm talkin' about!

963
01:03:11,002 --> 01:03:12,525
- Get a little present for ya.
- Who are you?

964
01:03:13,003 --> 01:03:14,994
- An NBA scout or something?
- This is gonna change your life.

965
01:03:15,172 --> 01:03:17,435
- Yeah?
- We'll be in touch.

966
01:03:19,709 --> 01:03:21,233
- Hey!
- Millie Dupree,

967
01:03:21,277 --> 01:03:24,507
- married 18 years to Hoppy Dupree.
- Hey, Hoppy...

968
01:03:24,613 --> 01:03:26,774
One of the city's
more unprincipled real estate agents.

969
01:03:27,182 --> 01:03:28,414
OK. Nice meeting you.

970
01:03:28,449 --> 01:03:30,610
As you know,
our Mr. Ringwald has set the bait.

971
01:03:30,785 --> 01:03:33,548
You know, Mr. Ringwald, if anybody
can help you walk that land deal

972
01:03:34,020 --> 01:03:35,787
through with the county, it'd be me.

973
01:03:35,822 --> 01:03:37,585
Owens and Strode are about to
close the snare as we speak.

974
01:03:38,057 --> 01:03:40,624
Is that convincing enough for you?

975
01:03:40,659 --> 01:03:44,116
- Freeze! Nobody move! Get down!
- FBI!

976
01:03:44,329 --> 01:03:45,694
Gentlemen, keep your hands on the table.

977
01:03:46,097 --> 01:03:47,497
Hoppy Dupree, you're under arrest.

978
01:03:47,532 --> 01:03:49,726
Hoppy Dupree,
you're in violation of federal code,

979
01:03:49,867 --> 01:03:52,165
attempting to bribe a government official.

980
01:03:52,336 --> 01:03:57,432
Mr. Kincaid, you wanna tell the court
just a little bit about your schooling?

981
01:03:57,840 --> 01:04:00,967
Well, not a whole lot to tell. I mean,
I guess most people got a college degree,

982
01:04:01,142 --> 01:04:03,905
and about the only thing I ever got
was the third degree.

983
01:04:05,679 --> 01:04:09,079
I appreciate your sense of humor, but
let's try to stay a little more on the topic.

984
01:04:09,215 --> 01:04:11,775
Stay more on Kyle Murphy
and his shop, Excalibur Guns.

985
01:04:12,151 --> 01:04:12,981
All right.

986
01:04:13,052 --> 01:04:15,315
Now, when you went over there
to get your shipment of guns,

987
01:04:15,587 --> 01:04:18,351
did Kyle Murphy ever say to you,
"Sorry, we're out,"

988
01:04:18,390 --> 01:04:21,187
or, "Now, why do you need 25
assault-style pistols in a month?"

989
01:04:21,425 --> 01:04:24,394
- Did he ever say anything like that?
- No, he never said anything like that.

990
01:04:24,429 --> 01:04:27,886
- He always had plenty of guns around.
- Gotcha. Thank you, Mr. Kincaid.

991
01:04:28,431 --> 01:04:30,295
Your witness.

992
01:04:30,366 --> 01:04:35,166
Mr. Kincaid, you were the one
who sold the gun used by Kevin Peltier

993
01:04:35,203 --> 01:04:37,398
in the brokerage house shootings.
Is that correct?

994
01:04:37,772 --> 01:04:41,223
Yeah, that is correct,
and I did a stretch for that, too.

995
01:04:41,258 --> 01:04:44,674
Now, to your knowledge,
was Vicksburg Firearms aware that

996
01:04:45,044 --> 01:04:47,478
you were out there reselling their product?

997
01:04:52,751 --> 01:04:54,581
No, not to my knowledge.

998
01:04:56,387 --> 01:04:59,413
He flipped on us.

999
01:05:02,425 --> 01:05:06,588
Or endorsed or fostered you
selling their guns in any way.

1000
01:05:07,062 --> 01:05:09,429
- Is that correct?
- Yeah, that's correct.

1001
01:05:09,464 --> 01:05:14,025
But I wish they had. I wish they had
endorsed me, like they did Kyle,

1002
01:05:14,201 --> 01:05:16,532
'cause I saw some pictures
he brought back from the trip he took

1003
01:05:16,870 --> 01:05:20,327
down to Cancun,
and, man, livin' it up down there.

1004
01:05:20,372 --> 01:05:22,806
I could've gone for some of that.
I wish they had sent me. I'd have been

1005
01:05:23,275 --> 01:05:25,640
on the beach right there with him,
looking at some big ol' butt thongs

1006
01:05:25,910 --> 01:05:28,174
and sipping on margaritas.
It would've been great.

1007
01:05:28,313 --> 01:05:31,110
- Simple answer, please.
- Simple answer is no...

1008
01:05:33,083 --> 01:05:37,416
And I want it on record here now, too.
I didn't have any idea what Peltier

1009
01:05:37,587 --> 01:05:40,021
was gonna do with that gun
when I sold it to him.

1010
01:05:41,923 --> 01:05:43,288
He was a wack job.
I wasn't about trying to get anybody hurt.

1011
01:05:43,425 --> 01:05:46,086
OK, I've retrieved some fragments.
I've got two things.

1012
01:05:46,160 --> 01:05:51,426
One: List of registered voters for Cincinnati.

1013
01:05:51,598 --> 01:05:53,691
Why would Nicholas Easter want
a list of registered voters in Cincinnati?

1014
01:05:53,833 --> 01:05:57,700
Because voter registration lists
lead to potential jury pools.

1015
01:05:57,903 --> 01:06:00,167
- What else you got?
- Well, this is from the drive's directory.

1016
01:06:00,339 --> 01:06:02,898
It's time-stamped
the day he got his jury summons.

1017
01:06:03,074 --> 01:06:06,236
He synchronized the data
on his drive to another device.

1018
01:06:08,345 --> 01:06:10,176
That's what you should've snagged.

1019
01:06:10,347 --> 01:06:13,838
Doyle, you're going back in, and this time
I want you to take Mr. Janovich.

1020
01:06:15,417 --> 01:06:17,884
Oh... Why do I have to take him?

1021
01:06:17,919 --> 01:06:20,285
Because Mr. Janovich can find anything.

1022
01:06:34,499 --> 01:06:38,093
I'm going for it.
Thank you, thank you. Watch this.

1023
01:06:38,369 --> 01:06:40,462
- Nick...
- Got one for me?

1024
01:06:51,380 --> 01:06:54,542
What's going on, Eddie?
You seem kind of gloomy. You all right?

1025
01:06:54,682 --> 01:06:59,686
Oh, no, no, I'm fine. You know,
I just got a lot on my mind, that's all.

1026
01:06:59,721 --> 01:07:02,120
You OK?
You wanna talk about it or anything?

1027
01:07:02,688 --> 01:07:04,747
I'm cool.

1028
01:07:04,890 --> 01:07:06,657
- Really.
- OK.

1029
01:07:06,692 --> 01:07:11,060
- Here's one more for you, Miss.
- Thank you very much.

1030
01:07:27,008 --> 01:07:28,998
Sorry, man.

1031
01:08:01,369 --> 01:08:03,234
I left my key inside.

1032
01:08:22,586 --> 01:08:24,076
Congratulations.

1033
01:08:26,756 --> 01:08:27,950
We got it. Let's go.

1034
01:08:30,525 --> 01:08:35,518
Janovich, you don't have to do this.
I said you don't have to do this!

1035
01:08:35,830 --> 01:08:37,695
Did you hear me?

1036
01:09:02,084 --> 01:09:04,882
Hey, guys. There's something
really wrong with Rikki.

1037
01:09:05,453 --> 01:09:07,182
I mean it. Come on!

1038
01:09:07,422 --> 01:09:09,583
See? Right there.

1039
01:09:14,661 --> 01:09:16,855
Come on, Rikki.

1040
01:09:19,465 --> 01:09:20,764
- Oh, God!
- Give me a towel.

1041
01:09:20,799 --> 01:09:23,666
Give me a paper towel. Paper towel, now!

1042
01:09:24,069 --> 01:09:25,763
Oh, that's nasty.

1043
01:09:25,836 --> 01:09:28,327
She's had five shooters.
It's no wonder.

1044
01:09:33,643 --> 01:09:35,406
- Call for help.
- What? She just had too much to drink.

1045
01:09:35,779 --> 01:09:37,439
- Call 911 now!
- OK, OK!

1046
01:09:38,947 --> 01:09:40,642
Damn!

1047
01:09:43,050 --> 01:09:44,745
I'm sorry.

1048
01:09:46,586 --> 01:09:48,587
I'm sorry, Rikki.

1049
01:09:48,622 --> 01:09:51,454
- Is she breathing, Nick?
- Yeah, she's gonna be OK, I think.

1050
01:09:54,960 --> 01:09:56,790
I can't believe this.

1051
01:09:56,828 --> 01:10:00,161
Damn lucky the place
is still here and no one was hurt.

1052
01:10:07,203 --> 01:10:09,034
People can't move back into this place.

1053
01:10:33,191 --> 01:10:37,559
They got to Rikki Coleman.
The woman was president of the PTA.

1054
01:10:37,627 --> 01:10:40,994
She bakes cookies for her kids. And
whatever that son of a bitch had on her,

1055
01:10:41,131 --> 01:10:45,328
he brought out the heavy lumber.
She swallowed a fistful of sleeping pills.

1056
01:10:45,634 --> 01:10:48,668
- Who's her replacement?
- Who's her...

1057
01:10:48,703 --> 01:10:51,934
Did you hear what I just said?
The woman almost killed herself tonight.

1058
01:10:52,407 --> 01:10:54,673
I'm sorry.

1059
01:10:54,708 --> 01:10:56,733
And they're putting the heat on the others.

1060
01:10:56,877 --> 01:11:00,744
Eddie Weese and Millie Dupree.
They torched my place tonight.

1061
01:11:00,980 --> 01:11:04,107
Well, at least we know
we have them motivated.

1062
01:11:04,416 --> 01:11:07,783
Oh. Here's the fun part.
They got the MP3 player.

1063
01:11:08,052 --> 01:11:09,985
Those files are encrypted.

1064
01:11:10,020 --> 01:11:13,352
Yeah, they're encrypted, but these guys
are good. They got teams of people.

1065
01:11:13,523 --> 01:11:17,516
You don't think they're gonna crack that?
We got three, four days, tops

1066
01:11:17,827 --> 01:11:19,851
before the walls cave in.

1067
01:11:19,995 --> 01:11:21,995
If we stay.

1068
01:11:22,030 --> 01:11:24,827
If we stay?
What do you mean, if we stay?

1069
01:11:25,066 --> 01:11:27,501
What are you saying to me, Nicky?
You saying to me you wanna run away?

1070
01:11:27,536 --> 01:11:29,297
- Is that what you're saying?
- Listen, Marlee.

1071
01:11:30,003 --> 01:11:32,301
- People are getting hurt.
- We're inside.

1072
01:11:32,405 --> 01:11:33,463
- People could get killed.
- No.

1073
01:11:33,672 --> 01:11:35,906
- No, I'm inside. You're on the street!
- Don't say that to me.

1074
01:11:35,941 --> 01:11:38,466
We're in this together, and it is your job -
it is our job - to protect ourselves.

1075
01:11:38,676 --> 01:11:40,075
Well, there's only so much
I can do, all right?

1076
01:11:40,511 --> 01:11:42,672
It's not just them that I worry about.

1077
01:11:46,984 --> 01:11:49,315
I can't protect you.

1078
01:11:57,759 --> 01:12:00,818
- Where are we with Rohr?
- He hasn't stepped up yet.

1079
01:12:01,028 --> 01:12:03,189
He will.

1080
01:12:03,364 --> 01:12:05,422
And Fitch wants to meet.

1081
01:12:06,599 --> 01:12:09,067
I mean, we knew I'd have to
go face-to-face with him.

1082
01:12:10,903 --> 01:12:14,895
It's OK. I can play him. I can...

1083
01:12:16,274 --> 01:12:21,370
- Set a date.
- OK.

1084
01:12:23,780 --> 01:12:25,838
We can't afford to lose any more jurors.

1085
01:12:26,215 --> 01:12:29,416
I know the math.

1086
01:12:29,451 --> 01:12:32,078
And we put Fitch on his heels right now.

1087
01:12:35,122 --> 01:12:38,683
This video is from a security camera
inside juror number nine's apartment.

1088
01:12:39,226 --> 01:12:41,625
Take a good look.

1089
01:12:44,030 --> 01:12:45,629
Oh, hi. The landlord let me in.

1090
01:12:45,664 --> 01:12:47,825
Lot of people had been
complaining about their cable,

1091
01:12:48,166 --> 01:12:51,032
but, uh, I checked yours out.
I don't think you have...

1092
01:12:51,068 --> 01:12:53,901
Gentlemen,
this malfeasance will not stand.

1093
01:12:54,138 --> 01:12:56,935
Now, I don't know which of you
this man was working for, and I don't care.

1094
01:12:57,140 --> 01:12:58,903
But you are lucky I can't make out his face,

1095
01:12:59,108 --> 01:13:01,109
because if I could, I'd be inclined to
turn this matter over to the DA's office.

1096
01:13:01,144 --> 01:13:02,508
- Outside.
- Your Honor, I move for a mistrial.

1097
01:13:02,844 --> 01:13:05,404
No, Mr. Cable.
There is not gonna be a mistrial here!

1098
01:13:05,447 --> 01:13:08,745
Motion denied!
Not because of maneuvers like this!

1099
01:13:13,586 --> 01:13:16,782
Now, for the remainder of this trial,
our jury is to be sequestered.

1100
01:13:17,022 --> 01:13:18,785
Bailiff, would you please inform...

1101
01:13:22,093 --> 01:13:23,092
- The guy in the video...
- Yeah?

1102
01:13:23,127 --> 01:13:24,890
Set fire to Nick Easter's place last night.

1103
01:13:25,263 --> 01:13:27,992
- What?
- Set fire to his place last night.

1104
01:13:28,165 --> 01:13:29,996
- How would you know that?
- Because I was there.

1105
01:13:34,537 --> 01:13:36,629
You were there?
You went to a juror's residence...

1106
01:13:37,038 --> 01:13:38,471
- Yes, yes.
... without asking me?

1107
01:13:38,540 --> 01:13:39,905
I'm gonna tell you what.
Wendall, listen to me.

1108
01:13:39,975 --> 01:13:41,737
- We're done. We're finished.
- That's what you pay me for.

1109
01:13:41,976 --> 01:13:44,706
No, no, no, no. You're a young punk,
and you should've asked me.

1110
01:13:44,945 --> 01:13:48,402
- You're fired. You're...
- Nick Easter is spinning this jury.

1111
01:13:49,849 --> 01:13:52,082
That's why Fitch ransacked his place.

1112
01:13:52,117 --> 01:13:54,745
There's some kind of twisted power play
going on between these two.

1113
01:13:55,120 --> 01:13:58,520
You're afraid, you go to the judge and
you tell him, they're gonna call a mistrial.

1114
01:13:58,622 --> 01:14:00,852
- The defense would love nothing more.
- That's right.

1115
01:14:01,125 --> 01:14:04,958
Why hasn't the defense
told the judge about Nick Easter?

1116
01:14:07,663 --> 01:14:10,188
Because they're really
gonna buy this verdict.

1117
01:14:12,333 --> 01:14:14,324
- Bastards.
- Celeste, behind you.

1118
01:14:15,670 --> 01:14:19,662
Hey, Celeste! Henry! Wham!

1119
01:14:19,806 --> 01:14:22,070
- Mr. Rohr...
- What's going on?

1120
01:14:22,242 --> 01:14:23,833
- That reporter just told me...
- Yeah?

1121
01:14:24,043 --> 01:14:25,109
She said something about a mistrial.

1122
01:14:25,144 --> 01:14:26,543
No, that's nonsense.
Don't worry about that.

1123
01:14:26,879 --> 01:14:28,847
- Well, she said the judge is gonna...
- No, no, now listen to me.

1124
01:14:28,882 --> 01:14:30,213
Let me explain something.
Everything's fine.

1125
01:14:30,248 --> 01:14:32,613
The judge has sequestered the jury.
You understand that?

1126
01:14:32,750 --> 01:14:33,910
And it's not unusual
when you have a heated trial.

1127
01:14:34,218 --> 01:14:37,051
- You said we had an excellent case.
- Yes.

1128
01:14:37,421 --> 01:14:39,285
When I met with you a year ago,
you said that

1129
01:14:39,389 --> 01:14:42,017
we could change the way
these people do business,

1130
01:14:42,225 --> 01:14:46,991
that my husband's life,
that all of those lives wouldn't be...

1131
01:14:47,362 --> 01:14:50,057
What?

1132
01:14:50,197 --> 01:14:52,688
- What's the matter, Celeste?
- I just need to hear it.

1133
01:14:55,702 --> 01:14:59,899
- Tell me we can win this.
- We can.

1134
01:15:00,072 --> 01:15:03,336
- We are gonna win it.
- OK.

1135
01:15:03,508 --> 01:15:06,272
- OK?
- Come on, Henry.

1136
01:15:06,344 --> 01:15:07,470
- Henry, you going to the park?
- Yeah!

1137
01:15:07,812 --> 01:15:10,439
I'll see you later.

1138
01:15:10,714 --> 01:15:13,409
How about we walk over
to Jackson Square...

1139
01:15:15,751 --> 01:15:19,550
I got Mr. Easter's list of potential jurors.
Boston, Pittsburgh and Cincinnati.

1140
01:15:19,754 --> 01:15:21,721
- What are the dates?
- Boston, 2000,

1141
01:15:21,756 --> 01:15:24,519
Pittsburgh and Brooklyn, '99,
and Cincinnati, '98.

1142
01:15:25,125 --> 01:15:28,561
They've been stalking gun cases.

1143
01:15:28,761 --> 01:15:31,321
Now they're in the house.

1144
01:15:31,531 --> 01:15:33,624
All right.

1145
01:15:33,800 --> 01:15:37,064
Amanda, I want the jury files
on all four cities. I want 'em tonight.

1146
01:15:37,169 --> 01:15:39,033
Line two. It's her.

1147
01:15:43,107 --> 01:15:44,773
Good afternoon, Marlee.

1148
01:15:44,808 --> 01:15:48,039
I see you and Mr. Easter
are keeping busy, busy, busy.

1149
01:15:48,311 --> 01:15:50,870
The Palace Cafe
on Canal Street. Downstairs,

1150
01:15:51,313 --> 01:15:53,713
main dining room. 15 minutes.

1151
01:16:01,321 --> 01:16:03,152
Thank you.

1152
01:16:24,507 --> 01:16:26,736
- Mr. Fitch?
- Marlee.

1153
01:16:26,908 --> 01:16:29,604
Good. I just won a bet with myself.

1154
01:16:29,778 --> 01:16:33,008
I figured you for blond.
Oh, please have a seat.

1155
01:16:35,248 --> 01:16:37,409
Nice mass¨¦, sequestering the jury.

1156
01:16:37,584 --> 01:16:40,950
Close ranks. Makes them harder to rea...

1157
01:16:50,261 --> 01:16:52,353
- Mr. Fitch?
- Yes?

1158
01:16:52,496 --> 01:16:54,760
- This is for you.
- Thank you.

1159
01:17:33,862 --> 01:17:40,027
- What's your price?
- 10 million. Nonnegotiable.

1160
01:17:42,502 --> 01:17:48,167
This is made out to cash. $500,000.

1161
01:17:48,374 --> 01:17:50,740
To walk away from my jury.

1162
01:17:51,477 --> 01:17:54,536
I wouldn't drop off
your dry cleaning for that.

1163
01:17:58,749 --> 01:18:01,817
You wanna ask Nick about that?

1164
01:18:01,852 --> 01:18:05,855
OK, OK. How do I know
that you can provide the verdict?

1165
01:18:05,890 --> 01:18:07,617
We got the jury sequestered, didn't we?

1166
01:18:08,056 --> 01:18:10,820
Look, we know you're
leaning on a couple of jurors,

1167
01:18:11,026 --> 01:18:13,721
but it's not enough.
You need nine warm bodies.

1168
01:18:16,497 --> 01:18:17,463
Nick Easter can deliver them.
He's very persuasive.

1169
01:18:17,498 --> 01:18:20,591
- You're such a good hustler.
- Mm. You're a good arsonist.

1170
01:18:20,733 --> 01:18:23,758
We both know what's at stake here.
You lose this one,

1171
01:18:23,936 --> 01:18:27,531
and every civil lawyer in the country
is gonna be holding out his hand.

1172
01:18:28,940 --> 01:18:30,703
This is mine.

1173
01:18:30,775 --> 01:18:35,575
You won't get off until the next stop.
It's the zoo.

1174
01:18:39,782 --> 01:18:41,875
I'm gonna give you
a second chance to take this.

1175
01:18:42,017 --> 01:18:48,352
I want you to consider it.
I think it's fair. I do. I really do.

1176
01:18:48,522 --> 01:18:54,187
Listen, Fitch, when you're
ready to pony up the ten,

1177
01:18:54,393 --> 01:18:58,056
email me at this address.
It's a free account, untraceable.

1178
01:18:58,130 --> 01:19:00,529
But then I'm sure you already knew that.

1179
01:19:37,095 --> 01:19:38,461
Here you are, sir.

1180
01:19:38,496 --> 01:19:42,158
OK. I get the being sequestered part,
but why put us all the way out here

1181
01:19:42,299 --> 01:19:43,857
- by the east Texas border?
- I know.

1182
01:19:44,167 --> 01:19:46,430
Hell, I can see Port Arthur
from the parking lot.

1183
01:19:46,502 --> 01:19:48,697
Does anyone know how Rikki's doing?

1184
01:19:48,838 --> 01:19:51,465
Oh, she was released
from the hospital about an hour ago.

1185
01:19:51,673 --> 01:19:54,005
- That's good.
- Lovely girl. She seemed fine to me,

1186
01:19:54,175 --> 01:19:56,642
but you never really know
what's going on with a person.

1187
01:19:56,977 --> 01:19:59,411
Could be her marriage, her church...

1188
01:19:59,446 --> 01:20:02,812
No, no, no. It was nothing like that.
Look, somebody got to that girl.

1189
01:20:02,982 --> 01:20:05,449
Oh, now that's hokum.
Why would anyone wanna get to her?

1190
01:20:05,617 --> 01:20:07,107
The same reason
they wanna get to any of us.

1191
01:20:07,486 --> 01:20:08,953
To influence the outcome of the trial.

1192
01:20:08,987 --> 01:20:11,546
- My Lord, are we safe?
- OK, here are your keys.

1193
01:20:11,922 --> 01:20:15,220
We are now.

1194
01:20:18,762 --> 01:20:21,629
Hoppy was arrested.

1195
01:20:21,664 --> 01:20:24,656
They said they would put him in jail for life

1196
01:20:25,167 --> 01:20:28,431
if I didn't vote in favor of the gun company.

1197
01:20:28,603 --> 01:20:31,332
It's OK, sweetheart.

1198
01:20:33,340 --> 01:20:35,705
- We'll talk about it in the morning.
- Thanks. Good night.

1199
01:20:36,108 --> 01:20:37,939
Good night.

1200
01:20:45,350 --> 01:20:48,216
Didn't take you for a man
who goes for the big girls, Easter.

1201
01:20:48,352 --> 01:20:51,650
You're just full of surprises, ain't you?

1202
01:20:51,888 --> 01:20:54,413
I'm fine, Frank. How are you?

1203
01:20:57,392 --> 01:21:00,793
- Can I get by?
- I've been watching you, Easter.

1204
01:21:01,329 --> 01:21:05,129
- From day one.
- I just wanna go to my room, Frank.

1205
01:21:05,232 --> 01:21:09,258
You're a real boy scout, aren't you?
Playing everybody.

1206
01:21:09,569 --> 01:21:12,303
Playing the judge. Playing Millie?

1207
01:21:12,338 --> 01:21:15,136
I've put up with guys like you my whole life.

1208
01:21:15,341 --> 01:21:20,334
Now, I want you to
understand something. OK?

1209
01:21:20,511 --> 01:21:27,039
I don't like my hand being forced.
You understand?

1210
01:21:30,219 --> 01:21:32,352
Get some sleep, Frank.

1211
01:22:06,048 --> 01:22:07,878
Mr. Rohr?

1212
01:22:13,019 --> 01:22:15,387
May I sit down?

1213
01:22:15,422 --> 01:22:18,083
- I know you're playing both sides.
- We are.

1214
01:22:19,959 --> 01:22:26,158
- May I sit down?
- Yeah, go on. Sit down. Sit down.

1215
01:22:31,602 --> 01:22:34,126
Now you tell me...

1216
01:22:34,270 --> 01:22:37,103
you tell me why I shouldn't
go to the judge, get a mistrial

1217
01:22:37,140 --> 01:22:40,631
and have that provocative little bottom
of yours thrown into jail.

1218
01:22:41,042 --> 01:22:43,944
You know how serious we are
about jury tampering down here?

1219
01:22:43,979 --> 01:22:45,809
- You wouldn't do that.
- Oh, don't you be so sure.

1220
01:22:46,247 --> 01:22:47,441
You wouldn't put Mrs. Wood
through the pain

1221
01:22:47,581 --> 01:22:49,309
and the expense of a mistrial.
She couldn't afford it.

1222
01:22:49,482 --> 01:22:52,315
You don't know anything about Celeste
Wood. You don't know what drives her.

1223
01:22:52,485 --> 01:22:54,748
Unlike you, young lady,
she's not in it for the money.

1224
01:22:54,920 --> 01:22:59,322
- And what are you in it for, Mr. Rohr?
- Oh, you're something, aren't you?

1225
01:22:59,490 --> 01:23:02,050
- What do you think I'm in it for?
- You tell me.

1226
01:23:02,493 --> 01:23:04,221
I'd like to get the law changed.

1227
01:23:04,261 --> 01:23:07,287
That's why you're here. But you need help.

1228
01:23:07,464 --> 01:23:10,489
It's up to you.
You can be a good boy, play by the rules...

1229
01:23:10,800 --> 01:23:14,335
or you can win a huge victory
for gun control. The choice is yours.

1230
01:23:14,370 --> 01:23:15,802
You know, this may come
as a surprise to you,

1231
01:23:16,371 --> 01:23:18,104
but I don't have to make that choice.

1232
01:23:18,139 --> 01:23:20,197
Don't tell me. Your whistle-blower
from Vicksburg is gonna testify

1233
01:23:20,340 --> 01:23:23,070
his company knows about the number
of guns that wind up on the black market.

1234
01:23:23,277 --> 01:23:24,266
That's right.
That's precisely what's gonna happen.

1235
01:23:24,644 --> 01:23:26,110
- Mr. Rohr.
- What?

1236
01:23:26,145 --> 01:23:27,874
What makes you think this witness
is ever gonna make it to the stand?

1237
01:23:28,314 --> 01:23:29,646
Because that whistle-blower came to us.

1238
01:23:29,681 --> 01:23:31,842
- Fitch is gonna get to him.
- We didn't call him. He came to us.

1239
01:23:31,983 --> 01:23:34,110
- He's your star witness.
- We didn't... Listen to me.

1240
01:23:34,252 --> 01:23:36,014
You don't know what this man's capable of!

1241
01:23:36,487 --> 01:23:38,079
Sweetheart! Hey! Hey! Hey!

1242
01:23:42,258 --> 01:23:46,522
He came to us. He's tucked away
right now. I'm not gonna tell you where.

1243
01:23:46,557 --> 01:23:50,787
The man, out of conscience,
cannot wait to get on that stand and testify.

1244
01:23:52,867 --> 01:23:55,028
Anyone can be gotten to.

1245
01:23:57,103 --> 01:24:00,401
What do you know? Hm?

1246
01:24:00,606 --> 01:24:04,133
How much are we talking about here?
What do you wanna bleed me for?

1247
01:24:04,310 --> 01:24:07,437
- Five, six, seven million?
- Ten million.

1248
01:24:07,612 --> 01:24:09,842
Ooh, that's a... big number.

1249
01:24:10,014 --> 01:24:12,811
That's a big number.

1250
01:24:12,883 --> 01:24:16,147
With Rankin Fitch in the game,
you can't win.

1251
01:24:19,121 --> 01:24:22,647
So if you just match his offer,
I'm gonna swing it your way. Good-bye.

1252
01:24:25,694 --> 01:24:28,856
Who hurt you?

1253
01:24:29,029 --> 01:24:32,429
Who made you this way? Come on.

1254
01:24:32,632 --> 01:24:35,624
You know what you're messing with here?
You know how important this is?

1255
01:24:50,080 --> 01:24:52,046
We've been poring over
those boxes of jury files.

1256
01:24:52,081 --> 01:24:56,347
We found this in the Cincinnati stack.
He used the name David Lancaster.

1257
01:24:56,684 --> 01:25:02,383
He was prospective juror number 54,
but he didn't make it on.

1258
01:25:02,756 --> 01:25:04,519
Call Doyle. Tell him to pack.

1259
01:25:04,591 --> 01:25:07,320
He's gonna be on
the first flight out to Cincinnati.

1260
01:25:07,593 --> 01:25:09,925
Let's see if Mr. Lancaster
has left any loose ends behind.

1261
01:25:10,096 --> 01:25:13,587
Everybody's got a dirty little secret.
Let's find out what his is.

1262
01:25:16,334 --> 01:25:17,633
Where the hell is he?

1263
01:25:17,668 --> 01:25:20,102
- He was in his room an hour ago.
- No, I know that. I know that.

1264
01:25:20,337 --> 01:25:21,736
Now, where is he? I thought
you had someone baby-sitting him.

1265
01:25:21,939 --> 01:25:24,498
- I'll keep trying the hotel.
- Find him. Get him here.

1266
01:25:25,775 --> 01:25:26,969
Mr. Rohr, is everything
all right down there?

1267
01:25:27,110 --> 01:25:29,304
Go find him.
Everything's fine, Your Honor.

1268
01:25:29,611 --> 01:25:32,429
Well, you called Mr. Kriegler
ten minutes ago.

1269
01:25:32,464 --> 01:25:36,075
- Could we show him in, please?
- Your Honor...

1270
01:25:36,110 --> 01:25:39,685
- Your Honor, may I approach?
- You may. Mr. Cable.

1271
01:25:42,621 --> 01:25:46,614
Your Honor, I'd like to request
a brief adjournment at this time.

1272
01:25:46,759 --> 01:25:50,626
- Where's your witness, Mr. Rohr?
- We're looking... we're looking into that.

1273
01:25:50,862 --> 01:25:52,352
Your request for an adjournment is denied.

1274
01:25:52,730 --> 01:25:54,458
Your Honor, this is my key witness.

1275
01:25:54,531 --> 01:25:57,056
Well, then you should have
kept a tighter leash on him.

1276
01:25:57,434 --> 01:25:58,599
Now, I told both of you,

1277
01:25:58,634 --> 01:26:00,192
I'm not gonna stand for
any more foolishness during this trial.

1278
01:26:00,403 --> 01:26:02,667
- We will proceed on schedule.
- But, Your Honor...

1279
01:26:02,972 --> 01:26:06,270
Now, Mr. Rohr... is there another
request you'd like to make at this time?

1280
01:26:06,374 --> 01:26:09,467
Your Honor, it would be highly prejudicial
to my client if we are not permitted to...

1281
01:26:09,643 --> 01:26:11,201
I will take that as a motion
for reconsideration.

1282
01:26:11,378 --> 01:26:14,073
- Your Honor.
- Denied. Call your next witness.

1283
01:26:14,213 --> 01:26:18,376
- Call your next witness, Mr. Rohr.
- I have no next witness.

1284
01:26:18,550 --> 01:26:21,178
If so, Mr. Rohr, then plaintiff rests.
Thank you, gentlemen.

1285
01:26:51,510 --> 01:26:57,573
Wendall Rohr. An overdue pleasure.
Rankin Fitch.

1286
01:26:58,984 --> 01:27:02,475
Nice suit. Very, um, "of the people,"

1287
01:27:02,519 --> 01:27:05,749
Yours is nicer. What would you call it?
"Gun lobby protecting its own"?

1288
01:27:06,055 --> 01:27:08,717
Oh! Swank shoes. Big tobacco?

1289
01:27:08,925 --> 01:27:11,518
Big alligator. Wrestled it myself.

1290
01:27:13,128 --> 01:27:15,527
Excuse me, we're cleaning up in here.

1291
01:27:17,531 --> 01:27:18,798
Am I gonna get beat up now, Mr. Rohr?

1292
01:27:18,833 --> 01:27:20,595
What'd you do to my witness?
Threaten his family?

1293
01:27:20,700 --> 01:27:23,567
Write him a check? Just curious
about what your technique is, Mr. Fitch.

1294
01:27:24,004 --> 01:27:28,167
Maybe he, uh, decided against biting
the hand that fed him these past few years.

1295
01:27:28,474 --> 01:27:30,304
You know exactly why he came to us.

1296
01:27:30,375 --> 01:27:34,539
Oh, please. Don't tell me you hung
your case on somebody's conscience.

1297
01:27:34,846 --> 01:27:36,244
I hung my case on my own conscience.

1298
01:27:36,446 --> 01:27:39,847
Oh. I get it now.

1299
01:27:39,883 --> 01:27:43,098
You are a moral man
living in a world of moral relativity.

1300
01:27:43,133 --> 01:27:46,314
- It's just so quaint, so precious.
- Hey! Don't do that.

1301
01:27:46,788 --> 01:27:50,417
This is about my witness, right?
This is about you messing with my client,

1302
01:27:50,691 --> 01:27:53,455
my case and the rules of law
that govern our country!

1303
01:27:53,761 --> 01:27:55,127
- Our country?
- Yes!

1304
01:27:55,162 --> 01:27:58,164
I didn't figure you for a patriot,
Mr. Rohr, what with your blatant disregard

1305
01:27:58,199 --> 01:28:00,893
for the people's right to bear arms.
You know, the Second Amendment?

1306
01:28:01,366 --> 01:28:04,426
Is that why you're doing this?
To protect the Constitution, is that it?

1307
01:28:04,636 --> 01:28:06,967
Of course not. I'm in it to win.
Just like you are.

1308
01:28:07,138 --> 01:28:08,604
- Because that's what I was hired to do.
- Uh-huh.

1309
01:28:08,639 --> 01:28:13,200
- Everything else is colored bubbles.
- Colored bubbles? Colored bubbles?!

1310
01:28:13,643 --> 01:28:18,341
A system that calls for 12 people to sit
and listen to testimony of witnesses, fella!

1311
01:28:18,547 --> 01:28:22,312
And that includes my witness,
who you've disappeared!

1312
01:28:22,517 --> 01:28:26,180
If you're relying on testimony
to win this case, you've already lost it, fella!

1313
01:28:26,386 --> 01:28:29,756
You think this jury cares anything
about negligent distribution?

1314
01:28:29,791 --> 01:28:31,848
- Product liability?
- You bet your ass they do!

1315
01:28:32,258 --> 01:28:35,091
Oh, most of 'em can't even say the words,
let alone understand the meaning.

1316
01:28:35,261 --> 01:28:38,194
You think your average juror
is King Solomon? No!

1317
01:28:38,229 --> 01:28:41,128
He's a roofer with a mortgage.
He wants to go home

1318
01:28:41,332 --> 01:28:44,994
and sit in his Barcalounger
and let the cable TV wash over him.

1319
01:28:45,369 --> 01:28:48,804
And this man doesn't give a single,
solitary droplet of shit

1320
01:28:49,005 --> 01:28:52,235
about truth, justice or your American way.

1321
01:28:53,909 --> 01:28:57,209
- They're people, Fitch.
- My point, exactly.

1322
01:28:57,244 --> 01:29:00,839
You don't have an idea of
what I've been talking about, do you, sir?

1323
01:29:02,549 --> 01:29:04,244
What do you hope to achieve if you win?

1324
01:29:04,851 --> 01:29:05,909
What do you mean?

1325
01:29:05,952 --> 01:29:08,511
You gonna bring Jacob Wood
back to life? No.

1326
01:29:08,687 --> 01:29:12,679
You just ensure that his wife
goes to the cemetery in a better car.

1327
01:29:12,757 --> 01:29:16,090
And that the heel that she snaps
on the way to the graveside

1328
01:29:16,260 --> 01:29:20,093
belongs to a $1200 shoe.
You get your name in the paper.

1329
01:29:20,297 --> 01:29:24,733
But Jacob Wood
and all the other gun violence victims

1330
01:29:25,067 --> 01:29:27,694
remain rotting in their crypts.

1331
01:29:31,573 --> 01:29:33,938
You know what, Fitch?

1332
01:29:34,108 --> 01:29:36,075
- You're gonna lose.
- I doubt it.

1333
01:29:36,110 --> 01:29:39,272
Well, maybe not this case,
maybe not the next, but someday,

1334
01:29:39,445 --> 01:29:42,437
you know, someday.
I've seen it before with guys like you.

1335
01:29:42,749 --> 01:29:45,048
- You wanna know why?
- Why?

1336
01:29:45,083 --> 01:29:47,108
Because you cannot
carry that much contempt

1337
01:29:47,252 --> 01:29:50,254
without it becoming malignant,
until you're gonna be all alone

1338
01:29:50,289 --> 01:29:52,814
in a room full of shadows,
and all you're gonna have

1339
01:29:53,223 --> 01:29:56,886
is the memories of all those
people's lives you have destroyed.

1340
01:29:57,260 --> 01:29:59,887
That's a good story, Wendall.

1341
01:30:00,095 --> 01:30:02,461
But it's just further proof
of why you can't beat me.

1342
01:30:02,631 --> 01:30:06,225
Because you may be right...

1343
01:30:06,267 --> 01:30:09,895
but the thing of it is, I don't give a shit.

1344
01:30:10,270 --> 01:30:14,399
What's more... I never have.

1345
01:30:16,308 --> 01:30:18,469
That suit.

1346
01:30:25,415 --> 01:30:27,713
- Good evening, Wendall.
- George. Mason.

1347
01:30:31,788 --> 01:30:35,518
So... what's this about?

1348
01:30:38,693 --> 01:30:43,129
I'm going to need access
to the firm's emergency reserve.

1349
01:30:44,265 --> 01:30:45,561
- Emergency reserve?
- Yeah.

1350
01:30:45,732 --> 01:30:48,530
- In what amount?
- Ten million.

1351
01:30:53,939 --> 01:30:59,570
Gentlemen, I've lost my footing in this trial.

1352
01:31:12,520 --> 01:31:15,079
- Yeah?
- I'm looking for a David Lancaster.

1353
01:31:15,422 --> 01:31:16,421
You a bill collector?

1354
01:31:16,456 --> 01:31:18,924
I work for Dodge and I track people down

1355
01:31:18,992 --> 01:31:21,619
who have rebates
that they forgot to apply for.

1356
01:31:21,961 --> 01:31:26,124
I don't know where he went.
No forwarding address.

1357
01:31:26,464 --> 01:31:28,864
Wish I did, though. Got this letter for him.

1358
01:31:28,900 --> 01:31:32,596
- Kept it right here.
- Wow. Can I... can I take a look at that?

1359
01:31:34,638 --> 01:31:36,764
"Jeff Kerr, care of David Lancaster. "

1360
01:31:36,940 --> 01:31:39,274
- You know Jeff Kerr?
- No.

1361
01:31:39,309 --> 01:31:42,573
"Professor Frank Phelan
at Bloomington University. "

1362
01:31:42,978 --> 01:31:45,469
Mm-hm.

1363
01:31:45,581 --> 01:31:50,643
You know I could maybe split the rebate
with you if you wanted to let me...

1364
01:31:50,884 --> 01:31:52,749
Jeff Kerr.

1365
01:31:54,487 --> 01:31:57,478
Jeff Kerr.

1366
01:31:57,623 --> 01:32:00,683
David Lancaster.

1367
01:32:00,826 --> 01:32:03,521
Lots of aliases.

1368
01:32:03,728 --> 01:32:06,890
Jeffrey Kerr.

1369
01:32:12,902 --> 01:32:15,393
Hello there, Jeffrey.

1370
01:32:18,840 --> 01:32:22,042
So, as CEO of Vicksburg
Firearms, you were unaware

1371
01:32:22,077 --> 01:32:24,306
that your company's product
was being sold illegally?

1372
01:32:24,612 --> 01:32:29,480
- Yes, sir. Absolutely, sir.
- Mr. Jankle, are you a family man?

1373
01:32:29,616 --> 01:32:34,143
Yes, sir, I am. I have a son
and I have an eight-year-old grandson.

1374
01:32:34,419 --> 01:32:36,444
Anything else you'd like to add?

1375
01:32:36,588 --> 01:32:38,680
The Second Amendment

1376
01:32:38,856 --> 01:32:41,726
permits every good citizen of
this country to keep and bear arms.

1377
01:32:41,761 --> 01:32:44,387
Now, we at Vicksburg Firearms,
we didn't write it.

1378
01:32:44,728 --> 01:32:47,355
But as long as it stands,
we're gonna continue

1379
01:32:47,429 --> 01:32:50,796
- to operate under its protections.
- Thank you, sir. Nothing further.

1380
01:32:50,933 --> 01:32:55,392
Counselor.
Mr. Rohr, do you care to cross?

1381
01:32:55,837 --> 01:32:58,669
- Yes, Your Honor.
- Proceed.

1382
01:33:02,575 --> 01:33:04,600
- Now, Mr. Jankle...
- Yes, sir.

1383
01:33:04,744 --> 01:33:06,878
The, uh, ad copy
used to sell the Performa 990,

1384
01:33:06,913 --> 01:33:10,143
- would you read it to the court, please?
- It's right there, Counselor.

1385
01:33:10,315 --> 01:33:13,078
Oh, but I think that the jury would like to
hear it from the head of the company here.

1386
01:33:13,384 --> 01:33:15,944
If you could say it,
starting with, "Also available in... "

1387
01:33:15,986 --> 01:33:18,419
"Also available in a print-resistant finish. "

1388
01:33:18,588 --> 01:33:22,388
I see. Now, who in your mind, sir, might
be eager to purchase a Performa 990

1389
01:33:22,759 --> 01:33:27,525
semiautomatic assault-type weapon
in a fingerprint-resistant finish?

1390
01:33:27,863 --> 01:33:29,428
- Anybody.
- Anybody?

1391
01:33:29,463 --> 01:33:32,728
Fingerprints are 90% water.
Water corrodes metal. Metal rusts.

1392
01:33:32,900 --> 01:33:35,993
Wouldn't it be more appropriate
then to use the word "rustproof"

1393
01:33:36,169 --> 01:33:38,932
than "fingerprint-resistant" when
you're trying to sell this weapon

1394
01:33:39,171 --> 01:33:40,433
- to the general public?
- Well, you know,

1395
01:33:40,472 --> 01:33:42,997
I didn't write the copy for that
advertisement, sir. I think you know that.

1396
01:33:43,275 --> 01:33:46,801
- Did you see it?
- Objection: Prejudicial.

1397
01:33:47,011 --> 01:33:49,478
- Sustained. Mr. Rohr.
- My apologies, Your Honor.

1398
01:33:49,880 --> 01:33:52,748
- Uh, now, Mr. Jankle?
- Yes, sir.

1399
01:33:52,783 --> 01:33:54,477
Tell me, sir, how much of
Vicksburg's profits come from

1400
01:33:54,917 --> 01:33:56,617
assault-type weapons, would you say?

1401
01:33:56,652 --> 01:33:58,915
Oh, I have to apologize to you, Mr. Rohr,
I do not know the answer.

1402
01:33:59,321 --> 01:34:01,016
I'm not familiar with that percentage.

1403
01:34:01,089 --> 01:34:04,353
But you are familiar with who buys
a weapon of this sort, aren't you, sir?

1404
01:34:04,525 --> 01:34:05,651
Well, the gun's intended
for recreational use.

1405
01:34:05,793 --> 01:34:07,260
Ah. For who?
Farmers or weekend sportsmen?

1406
01:34:07,461 --> 01:34:10,293
- Home protection.
- Or disgruntled day-traders?

1407
01:34:10,697 --> 01:34:11,925
- Objection, Your Honor.
- Sustained.

1408
01:34:12,098 --> 01:34:13,793
We sell these guns legally.

1409
01:34:13,833 --> 01:34:16,357
Oh, come on, Mr. Jankle, you know
that this gun is favored by criminals.

1410
01:34:16,535 --> 01:34:17,501
The statistics of your gun company
show that fact!

1411
01:34:17,536 --> 01:34:20,060
- Sir, that is up to law enforcement.
- I see. So that's not your problem.

1412
01:34:20,371 --> 01:34:21,838
- Why don't you just say it?
- Objection, Your Honor.

1413
01:34:22,006 --> 01:34:23,530
- Sustained.
- It is the government's responsibility!

1414
01:34:23,708 --> 01:34:25,265
Say it to the jury, sir,
that it's not your problem.

1415
01:34:25,475 --> 01:34:28,103
- Well, I...
- Say it to my client, Celeste Wood,

1416
01:34:28,311 --> 01:34:30,642
who lost her husband,
that it's not your problem.

1417
01:34:30,813 --> 01:34:33,338
- Tell it to the whole court...
- You amaze me, Mr. Rohr.

1418
01:34:33,549 --> 01:34:37,143
...that this Performa 990 with the
print-resistant finish is not your problem.

1419
01:34:37,352 --> 01:34:39,911
I stand on the Second Amendment,
goddammit!

1420
01:34:53,398 --> 01:34:55,661
Look, we've got damage-control strategies

1421
01:34:55,899 --> 01:34:57,764
and your testimony before cross
was more than strong.

1422
01:34:57,901 --> 01:35:02,064
Oh, you... you are the one that told me
denial was the route to take here.

1423
01:35:02,405 --> 01:35:04,270
- I was improperly prepped.
- Now, look, Mr. Jankle...

1424
01:35:04,474 --> 01:35:06,304
You get outta here.

1425
01:35:07,409 --> 01:35:10,400
Mr. Fitch, I looked into
the faces of those jurors,

1426
01:35:10,811 --> 01:35:11,877
I didn't see any friends sitting there.

1427
01:35:11,912 --> 01:35:13,345
Now where the hell are we
with securing my verdict?

1428
01:35:13,914 --> 01:35:15,915
It's a cat-and-mouse game.
We're about to change all that...

1429
01:35:15,950 --> 01:35:21,181
You just be a little more cat,
little less mouse.

1430
01:35:21,653 --> 01:35:24,816
Yes, sir.

1431
01:35:32,996 --> 01:35:35,330
- Hello, Marlee.
- Hello, Rankin.

1432
01:35:35,365 --> 01:35:39,027
I'm ready to make a deal.
I'll meet your terms.

1433
01:35:39,235 --> 01:35:42,829
I'll pay you the money.
I wanna meet Nick Easter.

1434
01:35:43,038 --> 01:35:46,370
Won't happen.
Wendall doesn't need to meet him.

1435
01:35:46,540 --> 01:35:50,055
And besides,
you know he's sequestered.

1436
01:35:50,090 --> 01:35:53,569
Well, fine.
How about his pal Jeffrey Kerr?

1437
01:35:54,047 --> 01:35:56,537
Now we meet or there's no deal.

1438
01:35:56,748 --> 01:36:00,115
Nonnegotiable. What's it gonna be?

1439
01:37:18,247 --> 01:37:23,275
I hope you don't mind. I was waiting
so long, I made a sandwich.

1440
01:37:30,023 --> 01:37:35,585
It's a fine piece, Nick.
Longines hunter case, circa 1908.

1441
01:37:35,862 --> 01:37:38,296
1910, actually.

1442
01:37:38,498 --> 01:37:40,297
- You filming this?
- No, no, no.

1443
01:37:40,332 --> 01:37:43,199
I couldn't use it.
Neither of us wants a mistrial.

1444
01:37:43,668 --> 01:37:47,002
- No.
- Can we walk?

1445
01:37:47,037 --> 01:37:51,200
Our bus is leaving at 8:00 am,
so that gives us a little time together.

1446
01:37:53,810 --> 01:37:56,607
Marlee, please come out of the kitchen.

1447
01:37:56,845 --> 01:37:59,938
I'm not here to hurt you.

1448
01:38:00,114 --> 01:38:04,914
Please, sweetheart, sit down. Relax.

1449
01:38:05,051 --> 01:38:07,485
So, what? I'm supposed to convince you
that I have them, right?

1450
01:38:07,554 --> 01:38:10,579
Oh, I-I think you've probably
got them, or you will have.

1451
01:38:10,923 --> 01:38:14,619
- I just wanna know why.
- Money.

1452
01:38:14,659 --> 01:38:17,423
Safer ways for a sharp kid
like you to make money.

1453
01:38:17,829 --> 01:38:20,126
What's the real reason?

1454
01:38:20,164 --> 01:38:23,327
Business, politics, sports -
you tell me what isn't rigged.

1455
01:38:23,667 --> 01:38:25,998
I mean, is there even such a thing
as an objective jury, Mr. Fitch?

1456
01:38:26,135 --> 01:38:27,435
Not if I can help it.

1457
01:38:27,470 --> 01:38:30,165
Then why should the lawyers
and guys like you make all the profit?

1458
01:38:30,372 --> 01:38:34,206
- You don't have much faith in the law.
- I'm agnostic.

1459
01:38:34,409 --> 01:38:36,968
I knew there was something
I liked about you.

1460
01:38:38,846 --> 01:38:40,676
And Marlee?

1461
01:38:42,415 --> 01:38:44,280
I'll leave it to her to tell you her reasons.

1462
01:38:44,417 --> 01:38:48,147
- That's not what I'm talking about.
- What are you talking about?

1463
01:38:48,453 --> 01:38:50,453
How well do you know her?

1464
01:38:50,488 --> 01:38:52,854
Did you really bring me
out here to talk about her?

1465
01:38:53,157 --> 01:38:54,920
What's this about?

1466
01:38:54,992 --> 01:38:59,792
Well, tomorrow's the big day
and we need a little insurance.

1467
01:39:01,564 --> 01:39:04,260
So... let's talk.

1468
01:39:04,466 --> 01:39:08,697
Come on out here, sit down, we'll talk.

1469
01:39:10,305 --> 01:39:13,398
I'll make you a sandwich.

1470
01:40:05,215 --> 01:40:06,248
I'm ready to pay the money.

1471
01:40:06,283 --> 01:40:09,479
I can have ten million
wire-transferred to the Caymans,

1472
01:40:09,785 --> 01:40:12,344
- subject to verification, of course.
- Of course.

1473
01:40:12,520 --> 01:40:15,255
But tell me, who have you got?

1474
01:40:15,290 --> 01:40:18,850
Fernandez, Deshazo, Grimes
and Dupree are in the bag.

1475
01:40:18,926 --> 01:40:21,190
Deets and Duke
pretty much follow the others.

1476
01:40:21,428 --> 01:40:24,954
- Herrera and Shaver are wild cards.
- No... Shaver's taken care of.

1477
01:40:25,465 --> 01:40:26,864
- Weese, too.
- Oh.

1478
01:40:27,066 --> 01:40:30,091
Herrera... I wouldn't worry about him.

1479
01:40:31,670 --> 01:40:37,107
- Are you gonna swing this my way?
- If you pay, yes. I will.

1480
01:40:37,275 --> 01:40:42,473
Good. Good. Tell Marlee
I'm ready to conclude our business.

1481
01:40:42,878 --> 01:40:44,971
Done.

1482
01:40:46,915 --> 01:40:51,613
- I must say I'm impressed, Mr. Kerr. I...
- Easter.

1483
01:40:52,120 --> 01:40:54,886
- Easter, correct.
- Yeah.

1484
01:40:54,921 --> 01:41:00,290
I didn't see you coming.
Obviously, I underestimated you.

1485
01:41:00,826 --> 01:41:03,885
And as a rule, I don't do that.

1486
01:41:05,263 --> 01:41:08,823
Make damn sure
you don't underestimate me.

1487
01:41:13,736 --> 01:41:16,971
I'm sure one of us will be in touch.

1488
01:41:17,006 --> 01:41:22,068
I have a feeling after tonight,
we're gonna be fast friends.

1489
01:41:42,894 --> 01:41:44,554
Who is it?

1490
01:41:47,664 --> 01:41:49,358
Who is this?

1491
01:41:50,733 --> 01:41:53,531
Bet you didn't expect to
hear from me so soon.

1492
01:41:53,969 --> 01:41:56,300
Is this a bad time?

1493
01:42:00,040 --> 01:42:02,907
- No, not at all.
- Oh, good.

1494
01:42:05,211 --> 01:42:09,494
Listen, I just wanted you to know
your associate with the accent...

1495
01:42:09,529 --> 01:42:13,777
he won't be coming home tonight.
At least he won't be walking home.

1496
01:42:14,286 --> 01:42:16,583
Aah!

1497
01:42:16,754 --> 01:42:19,814
And the price just went up.

1498
01:42:21,624 --> 01:42:24,650
15 million. Nonnegotiable.

1499
01:42:28,831 --> 01:42:30,695
Get some sleep, Rankin.

1500
01:42:30,932 --> 01:42:33,230
You're gonna need it.

1501
01:42:48,113 --> 01:42:51,843
- What's wrong?
- Nothing, I just, uh...

1502
01:42:54,284 --> 01:42:56,717
I just wanted to hear your voice.

1503
01:42:56,919 --> 01:43:01,617
- You sure?
- Yeah, I'm sure.

1504
01:43:01,823 --> 01:43:04,621
- I want you to get some sleep.
- Yeah.

1505
01:43:04,860 --> 01:43:07,851
I miss you. Good night.

1506
01:43:15,368 --> 01:43:17,801
Today, lawyers Wendall Rohr
and Durwood Cable

1507
01:43:17,870 --> 01:43:21,498
will have their last chances
to strike the winning blow for their client

1508
01:43:21,572 --> 01:43:24,200
in the case of
Celeste Wood v. Vicksburg Firearms.

1509
01:43:24,575 --> 01:43:26,376
Closing arguments will begin shortly.

1510
01:43:26,411 --> 01:43:28,071
The jury has now heard all the evidence
in this landmark case...

1511
01:43:28,445 --> 01:43:30,606
It's been
a very highly charged trial.

1512
01:43:30,747 --> 01:43:32,213
The jury has been sequestered
for some time.

1513
01:43:32,415 --> 01:43:33,507
- This is a nightmare, man.
- This could be the day...

1514
01:43:33,649 --> 01:43:35,116
...that they finally get this case
and begin their deliberations...

1515
01:43:35,284 --> 01:43:37,751
...a high-stake decision
will soon be in the hands of the jury.

1516
01:43:38,153 --> 01:43:40,520
This decision could be a quick one,

1517
01:43:40,555 --> 01:43:44,786
with the jury delivering a verdict
perhaps by day's end.

1518
01:43:51,564 --> 01:43:56,091
All Jacob Wood and those ten
other people did, all they did wrong...

1519
01:43:56,134 --> 01:44:00,502
on that Monday morning, was to go to
work.

1520
01:44:00,671 --> 01:44:03,901
And there's gonna be another
shooting and another shooting

1521
01:44:04,073 --> 01:44:09,339
and it's not gonna let up...
until we demand a change.

1522
01:44:09,678 --> 01:44:12,779
You heard Mr. Garland Jankle,

1523
01:44:12,814 --> 01:44:17,978
the chief CEO of Vicksburg Firearms,
sit in that chair

1524
01:44:18,118 --> 01:44:20,609
and say to you that
what we do with his guns

1525
01:44:20,987 --> 01:44:23,287
is not his problem, and he's right.

1526
01:44:23,322 --> 01:44:25,756
You can make it his problem
with your verdict.

1527
01:44:25,925 --> 01:44:32,625
You may for the first time
make gun violence...

1528
01:44:33,030 --> 01:44:36,265
the gun industry's problem.

1529
01:44:36,300 --> 01:44:43,932
If you do, you are going to
see fewer senseless deaths.

1530
01:44:45,974 --> 01:44:50,569
Like my client's husband, Jacob Wood.

1531
01:45:15,631 --> 01:45:18,725
- Make a big wish. Big wish!
- Big wish! Big wish!

1532
01:45:19,033 --> 01:45:20,091
Yeah!

1533
01:45:20,235 --> 01:45:22,703
That's my boy.

1534
01:45:41,919 --> 01:45:45,479
And the reality of how a big firm
actually practices law

1535
01:45:45,855 --> 01:45:48,187
just knocked some of the idealistic wind
right out of him.

1536
01:45:48,358 --> 01:45:52,384
The following spring,
after Gabby got her undergraduate degree,

1537
01:45:52,528 --> 01:45:55,394
- he dropped out and they left.
- Um, I'm sorry. Did you say Gabby?

1538
01:45:55,763 --> 01:45:59,494
Gabrielle Brandt. She was Jeff's girl
from back home in Gardner.

1539
01:45:59,734 --> 01:46:03,533
- Gardner, Indiana?
- Only one I know of.

1540
01:46:05,037 --> 01:46:07,198
Jacob Wood, a man in his prime,

1541
01:46:07,540 --> 01:46:11,854
leaves behind a wife and little boy.
I mean, the sadness of that,

1542
01:46:11,889 --> 01:46:16,169
the incomprehension, that gives way
to anger. We must even the score

1543
01:46:16,380 --> 01:46:19,247
and not just those
that are directly affected, but all of us.

1544
01:46:19,583 --> 01:46:23,313
We all experience these emotions.

1545
01:46:23,386 --> 01:46:29,119
Which is precisely why the law exists.
To do what is just.

1546
01:46:29,224 --> 01:46:32,056
Now these families grieve
and we all grieve for them.

1547
01:46:32,426 --> 01:46:35,486
But that is all the law will allow us to do.

1548
01:46:35,795 --> 01:46:38,320
Doyle, where the hell are you?

1549
01:46:38,364 --> 01:46:42,993
Headed for exit 245. Some
backwater town in central Indiana.

1550
01:46:43,201 --> 01:46:46,465
- But I think I got a good lead on the girl.
- Well, move it along.

1551
01:46:46,637 --> 01:46:50,038
The judge is about to
give this thing to the jury. Shit.

1552
01:46:50,908 --> 01:46:52,397
Ladies and gentlemen,
you've heard the evidence

1553
01:46:52,442 --> 01:46:56,105
and the arguments of counsel.
I will now give you some instructions,

1554
01:46:56,478 --> 01:46:58,241
after which we will hold recess for lunch,

1555
01:46:58,280 --> 01:47:00,407
and then you will begin
your deliberations.

1556
01:47:13,693 --> 01:47:15,558
- Wendall Rohr.
- Hello, Wendall.

1557
01:47:15,795 --> 01:47:17,060
Well, hello, Marlee.

1558
01:47:17,095 --> 01:47:20,087
Good to hear your voice.
Did you come up with the money?

1559
01:47:20,265 --> 01:47:24,664
You know, it's amazing
how easy it is to procure $10 million.

1560
01:47:24,699 --> 01:47:29,064
It's an interesting exercise.
$10 million. Like it was nothing.

1561
01:47:29,572 --> 01:47:32,267
But, as bad as I wanna win this case,

1562
01:47:32,507 --> 01:47:35,876
and I do...

1563
01:47:35,911 --> 01:47:39,879
after 35 years of doing this, you know...

1564
01:47:39,914 --> 01:47:43,508
I think it's more important that I can
rest my head on the pillow tonight.

1565
01:47:44,084 --> 01:47:45,949
What are you saying?

1566
01:47:46,019 --> 01:47:49,579
I will take my chances.
I'm not giving you a dime. Not a dime.

1567
01:47:49,755 --> 01:47:52,814
Well, I am sorry about this
'cause you're giving it to Fitch.

1568
01:47:53,191 --> 01:47:56,888
I'm giving it to Fitch?
I'm gonna take my chances.

1569
01:48:11,706 --> 01:48:12,798
Hello. Hello there.

1570
01:48:12,940 --> 01:48:14,669
- I'm sorry to bother you.
- Hi.

1571
01:48:14,975 --> 01:48:16,942
Um, I'm thinking of buying
this house down here.

1572
01:48:16,977 --> 01:48:19,740
My wife and I have a three-year-old
and another one on the way.

1573
01:48:19,979 --> 01:48:22,641
Do you think this is
a good neighborhood for kids?

1574
01:48:22,815 --> 01:48:26,375
Oh, this is a great neighborhood
for kids. I raised my girls here.

1575
01:48:26,985 --> 01:48:28,317
- Oh, really?
- I sure did.

1576
01:48:28,352 --> 01:48:30,855
This man's life was taken from him,
these other people were hurt and killed,

1577
01:48:30,890 --> 01:48:33,721
and the big-ass gun company
made it happen by flouting the law.

1578
01:48:34,357 --> 01:48:35,824
Come on, Loreen, where does it end, huh?

1579
01:48:35,859 --> 01:48:39,089
All these folks suing for profit.
What next? Have a heart attack,

1580
01:48:39,361 --> 01:48:41,989
sue McDonald's? Sue my grocery store
'cause you went in there

1581
01:48:42,464 --> 01:48:43,796
- and bought some pork chops...
- Go on!

1582
01:48:43,831 --> 01:48:46,095
...now you got clogged arteries?
- Oh, brother, you've been smoking.

1583
01:48:46,267 --> 01:48:49,702
Guess what? It is still our right to
keep and bear arms. Remember that.

1584
01:48:49,970 --> 01:48:53,235
Well, Mr. Fitch, are we
concluding our business today or not?

1585
01:48:53,607 --> 01:48:55,972
Could you hold
for just one minute, please?

1586
01:48:56,242 --> 01:48:57,266
One minute.

1587
01:49:00,779 --> 01:49:01,905
Doyle, give me something.

1588
01:49:02,147 --> 01:49:03,813
Well, I can't really talk
right now, sweetheart.

1589
01:49:03,848 --> 01:49:07,613
I met this nice lady and she's telling me
all about the neighborhood.

1590
01:49:08,085 --> 01:49:10,251
The jury has the case.

1591
01:49:10,286 --> 01:49:12,720
- There could be a verdict any second.
- I hear you, sweetheart.

1592
01:49:12,889 --> 01:49:16,058
I tell you what. I'll call you back
as soon as I get on the road, OK?

1593
01:49:16,093 --> 01:49:19,118
- Doyle.
- I love you, too. All right. Bye.

1594
01:49:19,294 --> 01:49:24,424
Word just in. Rohr's not paying. It's yours.

1595
01:49:24,931 --> 01:49:28,667
Marlee? Unhappy news, I hear.

1596
01:49:28,702 --> 01:49:31,101
Rohr has taken himself out of the game.

1597
01:49:31,236 --> 01:49:34,864
I thought maybe you might be inclined
to make a reduction in your selling price.

1598
01:49:35,073 --> 01:49:38,600
Look, Rankin, in 30 seconds
you're still gonna lose. Understand?

1599
01:49:39,110 --> 01:49:42,203
Now, do we have a deal or don't we?

1600
01:49:46,248 --> 01:49:48,783
We have a deal. I'm wiring the money.

1601
01:49:48,818 --> 01:49:52,981
Congratulations, Mr. Fitch.
You just bought yourself a verdict.

1602
01:49:53,321 --> 01:49:57,450
It's not our job to change the law.
It is our duty to serve it.

1603
01:49:57,591 --> 01:50:01,048
And the law says this guy Kevin Peltier
was responsible.

1604
01:50:01,427 --> 01:50:02,560
- Not the gun company.
- Right.

1605
01:50:02,595 --> 01:50:04,688
Yes, we as a people,
we are meant to serve the law,

1606
01:50:05,098 --> 01:50:07,177
but the law is also
meant to serve the people.

1607
01:50:07,212 --> 01:50:09,258
- That's right.
- I'm with you, Frank, all right?

1608
01:50:33,954 --> 01:50:36,251
Whoa, whoa. Ladies and gentlemen, whoa.

1609
01:50:36,422 --> 01:50:39,016
I think a preliminary vote here
would help us along

1610
01:50:39,658 --> 01:50:41,357
if nobody has anything new to say.

1611
01:50:41,392 --> 01:50:43,087
- Let's vote.
- I'd like to say something.

1612
01:50:43,628 --> 01:50:45,721
Couldn't be easy.

1613
01:50:45,897 --> 01:50:48,262
Hey, Frank, let him speak.

1614
01:50:52,435 --> 01:50:54,995
Well, I hope you like
lots of sugar in your iced tea.

1615
01:50:55,538 --> 01:50:57,767
- Oh, yes, ma'am, I surely do.
- Good.

1616
01:50:57,939 --> 01:51:00,965
- Thank you.
- You're welcome.

1617
01:51:01,076 --> 01:51:03,703
- Are these your daughters?
- Mm-hm.

1618
01:51:03,944 --> 01:51:06,208
Gabrielle and Margaret.

1619
01:51:06,313 --> 01:51:09,907
They were Irish twins.
You know, just one grade apart.

1620
01:51:10,183 --> 01:51:12,275
Mm-hm.

1621
01:51:13,385 --> 01:51:15,649
Forgive me.

1622
01:51:15,821 --> 01:51:19,051
It's still hard to talk about.

1623
01:51:19,223 --> 01:51:20,952
I'm sorry?

1624
01:51:22,759 --> 01:51:24,454
Well, it was bad enough losing Margaret,

1625
01:51:24,661 --> 01:51:27,390
but it's been so long
since I've spoken to Gabby,

1626
01:51:27,963 --> 01:51:29,930
I just feel like I've lost them both.

1627
01:51:29,965 --> 01:51:36,426
Well, um, what happened?
Uh, how did Margaret pass away?

1628
01:51:36,771 --> 01:51:40,797
You're not from around here.
You wouldn't know about the shooting.

1629
01:51:41,141 --> 01:51:44,243
I don't think this case is about guns.
I think this case is about the law,

1630
01:51:44,278 --> 01:51:46,734
and like Frank says,
it's about serving the law.

1631
01:51:47,246 --> 01:51:50,305
But guns are involved and I don't know
anybody in the room or in the world

1632
01:51:50,515 --> 01:51:53,482
- who doesn't feel strongly about guns...
- Oh, please.

1633
01:51:53,517 --> 01:51:56,451
- What's wrong, Frank?
- I can guess which way you lean.

1634
01:51:56,821 --> 01:51:58,219
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

1635
01:51:58,254 --> 01:51:59,687
You've had an agenda
the whole time you've been here.

1636
01:51:59,756 --> 01:52:01,849
- You got that right.
- You're the plaintiff's boy.

1637
01:52:01,991 --> 01:52:03,855
Hey, come on, Frank.
What are you saying?

1638
01:52:04,193 --> 01:52:05,660
- It's the truth.
- What are you saying?

1639
01:52:05,761 --> 01:52:07,592
- Let him speak.
- Well, what's the problem here?

1640
01:52:08,163 --> 01:52:10,445
They made a good product.
It worked. Case closed.

1641
01:52:10,480 --> 01:52:12,728
It's a little more complicated
than that, Frank.

1642
01:52:16,503 --> 01:52:19,403
A boy, James Pratt,

1643
01:52:19,438 --> 01:52:21,770
showed up at the high school
with some guns that he bought

1644
01:52:21,941 --> 01:52:25,637
off the street in Indianapolis
and he just started firing.

1645
01:52:28,645 --> 01:52:32,314
Yeah, I'm sorry the lady lost her husband.
I am, but that's life.

1646
01:52:32,349 --> 01:52:36,341
I mean, I know a lot of other people had
bad times, but they never sued anybody.

1647
01:52:36,853 --> 01:52:38,952
- Like who?
- Like buddies of mine. Lost their arms,

1648
01:52:38,987 --> 01:52:42,479
- their legs. Never got a thin dime.
- And they deserved better. You're right!

1649
01:52:42,624 --> 01:52:44,887
You bet I'm right. We're done here, Easter.

1650
01:52:45,125 --> 01:52:47,821
- Could we vote, please?
- That's not good enough.

1651
01:52:48,395 --> 01:52:50,191
- Let's vote.
- That's not good enough.

1652
01:52:50,363 --> 01:52:53,547
I wanna talk about the law.
I wanna talk about Celeste Wood.

1653
01:52:53,582 --> 01:52:56,730
I got a call from the school
and they said that something

1654
01:52:56,802 --> 01:53:00,066
had happened to Margaret.
That she had died.

1655
01:53:01,873 --> 01:53:04,273
- She doesn't deserve that money.
- Well, maybe she does,

1656
01:53:04,474 --> 01:53:06,601
maybe she doesn't.
But she deserves a few hours of our time.

1657
01:53:07,043 --> 01:53:08,543
- Can we vote, please?
- Yeah.

1658
01:53:08,578 --> 01:53:09,942
- Tell me why. Tell me why.
- I wanna vote.

1659
01:53:10,246 --> 01:53:12,111
Anybody else wanna vote?
Come on, I wanna vote.

1660
01:53:12,581 --> 01:53:13,411
- Tell me why.
- Well, I wanna vote.

1661
01:53:13,649 --> 01:53:14,648
- Does anyone else here...
- Let's vote.

1662
01:53:14,682 --> 01:53:16,411
- How does it work, Frank?
- Pipe down, Easter.

1663
01:53:16,851 --> 01:53:18,716
- Tell me why.
- We all got problems here, don't we?

1664
01:53:18,820 --> 01:53:21,481
- Amen.
- All of us. But we don't get paid for it.

1665
01:53:22,022 --> 01:53:23,155
- She suffered!
- Hell, I suffered.

1666
01:53:23,190 --> 01:53:26,625
I worked my ass off 12 years
of crummy jobs for shit pay!

1667
01:53:26,659 --> 01:53:28,786
I have never asked anybody for a handout.

1668
01:53:29,262 --> 01:53:31,396
- She's not asking for a handout.
- That is bullshit and you know it.

1669
01:53:31,431 --> 01:53:33,990
She was with Jeff.
That's Gabby's boyfriend.

1670
01:53:34,666 --> 01:53:38,157
He told me that my girl, she froze.

1671
01:53:38,369 --> 01:53:43,004
She just stood there. It took seconds.

1672
01:53:43,039 --> 01:53:48,806
Jeff just kept saying, "I couldn't
reach her. I couldn't pull her down. "

1673
01:53:53,047 --> 01:53:57,244
He always thought there was something
that he could have done.

1674
01:53:58,618 --> 01:54:00,881
So what are we gonna tell Celeste Wood?

1675
01:54:01,220 --> 01:54:02,448
I don't know. I don't know
what we're gonna tell her.

1676
01:54:02,688 --> 01:54:05,356
"Life isn't fair"? Write that on a postcard?
Is that the best we've got?

1677
01:54:05,391 --> 01:54:08,951
I'll tell you what else. That lawyer can
show me all the home movies he wants.

1678
01:54:09,193 --> 01:54:11,592
I deserve a hell of a lot more
for what I've been through

1679
01:54:12,095 --> 01:54:14,064
than that woman out there.
I mean, forget her!

1680
01:54:14,099 --> 01:54:15,827
- What about the law?
- Fuck the law!

1681
01:54:16,198 --> 01:54:19,895
I don't care if the gun company
gave that guy an M-16

1682
01:54:19,969 --> 01:54:23,995
with his morning donut.
I'm not giving her a cent!

1683
01:54:27,608 --> 01:54:33,637
- That's bullshit, Frank. Bullshit.
- Who's with Frank?

1684
01:54:36,548 --> 01:54:40,075
Yes, I'd like to report a robbery
in progress at Mardi Gras Costumes,

1685
01:54:40,552 --> 01:54:43,952
6510 Charter Street.

1686
01:54:45,122 --> 01:54:48,579
Maybe some of you
are afraid or intimidated.

1687
01:54:48,992 --> 01:54:51,688
Maybe some of you
are just out for yourselves.

1688
01:54:54,229 --> 01:54:59,325
Frank is right about me.
I'm the worst offender here.

1689
01:54:59,400 --> 01:55:03,460
I made up my mind about this case
before I stepped through that door.

1690
01:55:05,739 --> 01:55:09,156
But this trial's not about me.
It's not about you.

1691
01:55:09,191 --> 01:55:12,573
Now we owe it to Celeste Wood
to sit in this room

1692
01:55:13,144 --> 01:55:19,946
and deliberate the facts of the case
for as long as it takes.

1693
01:55:21,618 --> 01:55:24,347
Now if you don't mind,
there's some testimony I'd like to review.

1694
01:55:27,523 --> 01:55:29,217
Mr. Fitch?

1695
01:55:29,458 --> 01:55:30,982
Yeah?

1696
01:55:31,159 --> 01:55:33,426
- Don't wire the money.
- What? What's going on?

1697
01:55:33,461 --> 01:55:38,056
- Where are you?!
- I'm in Gardner. Gardner, Indiana.

1698
01:55:38,598 --> 01:55:40,896
- It's a setup.
- Gardner.

1699
01:55:44,036 --> 01:55:48,105
- Get it open!
- Oh, shit. We got company.

1700
01:55:48,139 --> 01:55:51,006
Take the network down now.
OK, people, pack it up, tear it down.

1701
01:55:51,209 --> 01:55:54,803
Now. This is not a test.
Clear your drives, wipe the screens.

1702
01:55:55,012 --> 01:55:57,344
Let's go. Let's go. Box all photos and files.

1703
01:55:57,547 --> 01:56:00,913
No monitors. Leave the monitors.
Code four, people.

1704
01:56:01,484 --> 01:56:02,678
- Mr. Fitch?
- 30 seconds!

1705
01:56:02,852 --> 01:56:05,081
Mr. Fitch, we really need to go, sir.

1706
01:56:07,122 --> 01:56:08,121
20 seconds!

1707
01:56:08,156 --> 01:56:09,622
Lamb, what the hell happened in Gardner?

1708
01:56:10,290 --> 01:56:11,857
1989. Gardner v. Blackwell Arms.

1709
01:56:11,892 --> 01:56:14,257
Town sued the gun manufacturer,
town lost.

1710
01:56:14,460 --> 01:56:16,792
Town went bankrupt.
Fitch worked the case.

1711
01:56:17,330 --> 01:56:20,696
You got it? Fitch worked the case.

1712
01:56:30,340 --> 01:56:33,434
...to hear the jury's decision.

1713
01:56:51,190 --> 01:56:54,886
Ladies and gentlemen of the jury,
have you reached a verdict?

1714
01:56:59,897 --> 01:57:00,696
We have, Your Honor.

1715
01:57:00,731 --> 01:57:03,427
Would you hand it to the bailiff, please?

1716
01:57:07,870 --> 01:57:10,463
Thank you, Bailiff.

1717
01:57:20,213 --> 01:57:23,740
In the matter of
Celeste Wood v. Vicksburg Firearms,

1718
01:57:23,850 --> 01:57:27,512
the jury finds for the plaintiff Celeste Wood.

1719
01:57:27,553 --> 01:57:30,783
They award special damages
in the amount of $1 million

1720
01:57:30,955 --> 01:57:33,219
and general damages
in the amount of $110 million.

1721
01:57:35,625 --> 01:57:38,719
This court is still in session.

1722
01:57:38,895 --> 01:57:41,522
Court is in session. Order, please. Order.

1723
01:57:41,797 --> 01:57:45,426
- That's bullshit!
- That's enough. Court's adjourned.

1724
01:58:21,263 --> 01:58:24,026
When Judge Harkin read this verdict,

1725
01:58:24,232 --> 01:58:27,359
a spontaneous round of applause
from the entire courtroom. Obvious support

1726
01:58:27,801 --> 01:58:33,466
for the teary-eyed widow who waged
war on the entire gun industry...

1727
01:58:40,111 --> 01:58:43,307
No matter what the
anti-gun lobbyists say, or the NRA says,

1728
01:58:43,680 --> 01:58:48,707
on this day, in this courtroom,
the final word was given by the jury.

1729
01:58:48,818 --> 01:58:50,479
There have
been other lawsuits filed

1730
01:58:50,920 --> 01:58:52,648
against gun manufacturers in the past

1731
01:58:52,821 --> 01:58:57,814
and juries have traditionally
sided with the gun industry, until today.

1732
01:58:57,992 --> 01:59:02,258
The $110 million award, which
the jury handed Celeste Wood today,

1733
01:59:02,429 --> 01:59:04,123
could cripple the gun industry.

1734
01:59:04,597 --> 01:59:09,056
And to say it's been an uphill battle
for Mr. Rohr is an understatement.

1735
01:59:09,101 --> 01:59:12,400
In fact, you might say it's been
a David and Goliath battle.

1736
01:59:12,471 --> 01:59:17,601
But today,
one gun industry giant has fallen.

1737
01:59:21,811 --> 01:59:24,643
Ten years, Fitch.

1738
01:59:30,985 --> 01:59:34,579
They're gonna appeal, the gun company.
You know that, right?

1739
01:59:35,055 --> 01:59:39,081
Of course. But not with you.

1740
01:59:40,326 --> 01:59:42,326
You show up on another case, any case,

1741
01:59:42,361 --> 01:59:47,161
even a contested parking ticket,
this gets faxed to the IRS.

1742
01:59:47,299 --> 01:59:50,665
And the Justice Department,
the Federal Board of Judicial Review.

1743
01:59:50,834 --> 01:59:56,294
You're out. Retired as of today.

1744
01:59:56,773 --> 01:59:59,502
Gardner, Indiana.

1745
02:00:01,276 --> 02:00:04,711
- School shooting.
- He remembers.

1746
02:00:04,912 --> 02:00:06,846
Blackwell Arms.

1747
02:00:06,881 --> 02:00:10,577
The town really thought
they had that one, didn't they?

1748
02:00:12,819 --> 02:00:14,649
We did have it.

1749
02:00:14,887 --> 02:00:16,887
Now what?

1750
02:00:16,922 --> 02:00:20,254
What on earth are you gonna do
with all that money, huh?

1751
02:00:20,358 --> 02:00:23,953
- $15 million, that changes people.
- Yeah, well, that's what we're hoping.

1752
02:00:24,262 --> 02:00:28,031
See, back home, the victims' families
could really use that money.

1753
02:00:28,066 --> 02:00:31,124
- Good-bye, Fitch.
- Well, wait, wait, wait, wait, wait.

1754
02:00:31,767 --> 02:00:33,668
How did you swing 'em, huh?

1755
02:00:33,703 --> 02:00:36,728
How'd you swing 'em your way?
I hear you got ten votes.

1756
02:00:36,905 --> 02:00:40,396
- How'd you do that?
- I didn't swing anybody.

1757
02:00:40,541 --> 02:00:44,307
I just stopped you from stealing the thing.

1758
02:00:44,678 --> 02:00:49,749
We let 'em vote their hearts.
That means you lose. Enjoy your drink.

1759
02:00:49,784 --> 02:00:53,309
What, do you think you can
just walk away from this? Huh?

1760
02:00:53,552 --> 02:00:56,281
And I'm betting that the two of you,
you can't stop!

1761
02:00:56,454 --> 02:00:59,821
Because if you did, what would you have?!

1762
02:01:00,423 --> 02:01:02,653
Nothing!

1763
02:01:02,826 --> 02:01:04,623
Nothing!

1764
02:01:50,931 --> 02:01:54,934
That's why we're gonna have lunch.
We're celebratin', that's why.

1765
02:01:54,969 --> 02:01:56,696
- Do you know why we're celebrating?
- Yeah.

1766
02:01:57,403 --> 02:01:58,636
We are very happy today.

1767
02:01:58,671 --> 02:02:00,866
Celeste, Henry, go on inside,
get me an extra order of cornbread.

1768
02:02:01,407 --> 02:02:03,874
Get 'em that table, Milton.
Just gonna be one minute.

1769
02:02:04,108 --> 02:02:05,939
- You got it.
- Thank you.

1770
02:02:38,536 --> 02:02:41,369
I wanna go home.

1771
02:02:44,040 --> 02:02:48,066
OK. Let's go.
